00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:26.840
AMY BANZAERT: All right.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.147
Everyone has their candles lit?

00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:28.730
AUDIENCE: Yep.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.990
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.803
So you can start the quiz.

00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:34.268
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:00:51.298 --> 00:00:52.840
AMY BANZAERT: All
right, so time's up

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.050
so you can just turn--

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.880
hand all the quizzes down
the lanes to get to me.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.270
That would be great.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.060
Given the amount of
background and expertise

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.070
you have in this
area, I'm clearly

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:13.030
not going to grade these
quizzes with a lot of cruelty--

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.100
so I don't know how
to put it, but they'll

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.230
be graded leniently
so don't stress too

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.600
much about getting it through.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.910
The real idea was
to call it a quiz so

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.730
that you had some
motivation to do the work

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.590
and then to
experience doing work

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.110
with a variety of
different lighting

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:34.060
situations, all of which are
pretty limited in function

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.350
compared to what you're used to.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.940
So we're going to do part
of the lecture in the dark,

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.480
so you can keep your
candles and lanterns

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.580
and whatever you want
on because that's

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.870
all we've got for right now.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.230
I would ask that you don't
use your flashlights too much

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.560
because they're really noisy.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.600
So first I just want
to talk about what

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.420
it was like to use each of
the different lighting sources

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.350
and light to have to do
work, doing all of them--

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.100
using them.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.617
We started with
just candle light.

00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:13.700
AUDIENCE: Quite difficult.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.783
AMY BANZAERT: So candle
light was quite difficult?

00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:17.655
AUDIENCE: Very difficult.

00:02:17.655 --> 00:02:18.780
AUDIENCE: There's just one.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.460
AUDIENCE: It's a very
limited light source,

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:25.335
low light distribution,
and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:36.930
It's actually very low
lux so it's inconvenient.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.353
It's definitely a way to
go without electricity.

00:02:40.353 --> 00:02:41.770
AUDIENCE: When
your family is sick

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.210
and you're huddling
really close together.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.290
AMY BANZAERT: And just make
sure you speak loud enough

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.730
so everyone can hear.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.040
When people are sick,
everyone's huddling together.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:48.610
Yeah.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.004
Curt, did you have a hand up?

00:02:50.004 --> 00:02:51.796
AUDIENCE: I'm not sure
if Carter said this,

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:53.020
but it flickered a lot.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.395
AMY BANZAERT: It
flickered a lot.

00:02:54.395 --> 00:02:55.456
Yeah.

00:02:55.456 --> 00:02:58.948
AUDIENCE: When the flame is
more protected, that helped.

00:02:58.948 --> 00:02:59.740
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.916
What else about the
second candle helped?

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:06.498
AUDIENCE: The diffuse light.

00:03:06.498 --> 00:03:07.290
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.165
AUDIENCE: I like that
it helped diffuse light

00:03:09.165 --> 00:03:12.547
and it made it a lot
easier to-- or distribute

00:03:12.547 --> 00:03:13.630
the light a little better.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:14.020
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:14.650
Absolutely.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:20.620
So the second candle has the
diffusing aspect as well as

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.570
the wind protection,
both of which

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:26.560
add a lot to their
functionality.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.470
And then next I gave you
guys some aluminum foil.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.782
How did you use it and
did you find it useful?

00:03:32.782 --> 00:03:33.740
Let's go to each group.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.727
So how did you
guys use it, Missy?

00:03:35.727 --> 00:03:37.810
AUDIENCE: We put it under
our two handles and kind

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.080
of just spread out the light
a little bit along the table

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:44.607
so since we were all crouched
over anyway, it really helped.

00:03:44.607 --> 00:03:45.690
AMY BANZAERT: It did help?

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:46.190
Yeah.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.610
And how about you guys?

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.042
AUDIENCE: We put
it up like this.

00:03:49.042 --> 00:03:50.750
AMY BANZAERT: And did
you find it helped?

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.375
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:03:51.375 --> 00:03:52.670
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.360
And then back there?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.468
AUDIENCE: We reflected it
towards the writing area.

00:03:58.468 --> 00:03:59.260
AMY BANZAERT: Cool.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.770
And how about you guys?

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.617
AUDIENCE: We did the
same thing, put it over.

00:04:03.617 --> 00:04:04.450
AMY BANZAERT: Great.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.400
And you guys actually
started with some tinfoil

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.733
that I guess you got from lunch.

00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:09.490
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.040
AMY BANZAERT: Nice.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.090
Yeah.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.649
And then we added the
flashlights, I think.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:18.850
Is that right?

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.160
And some of you guys used
them and some of you didn't.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.670
So for those who used it,
what was good about it?

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:30.780
Yeah?

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.800
AUDIENCE: It was very
bright, very concentrated.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.650
It was nice.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.710
Just the life span
of it was very short,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.379
so it was a lot
of work that need

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:44.440
to be put into it to get the
light that you desire for this.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.498
So short-term, sure,
it's nice use, but--

00:04:46.498 --> 00:04:48.040
AMY BANZAERT: But
it's a lot of work.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:48.885
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

00:04:48.885 --> 00:04:51.510
So maybe one person would do it
and they'll just write the quiz

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.108
and then swap.

00:04:52.108 --> 00:04:52.900
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.130
Yeah.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.130
Well, you guys liked it.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.785
Jessica, yeah?

00:04:57.785 --> 00:04:58.410
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.990
I have to agree with
what Carter said.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.820
Also when I started
with the candles,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.710
the first one was annoying, the
second one got so much better.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.160
By the time that we had the
lantern that you provided,

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.330
the flashlight that you provided
us, plus our three lanterns,

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.205
I was like, there's so
much excess light here

00:05:12.205 --> 00:05:13.068
we don't need.

00:05:13.068 --> 00:05:13.860
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.950
So it's kind of phenomenal
how, at this point,

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.690
I imagine most of
us are relatively

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.520
comfortable in this
level of light.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.920
Whereas if you had
walked into the classroom

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.510
in this level of light, you
would not have been thrilled.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.795
But starting with just a candle,
having all these light sources

00:05:30.795 --> 00:05:32.100
is actually decent.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.910
However, can you imagine
studying for your final--

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.130
during finals week using
this amount of light

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:39.450
to comfortably study?

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.960
Would this be-- would
you opt for this?

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:43.890
Probably not.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.480
I sure wouldn't.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.210
I'd want a whole lot more light
to read comfortably and write

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.220
comfortably.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.440
Or if you were sewing
or cooking, et cetera.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.250
So this is both a
surprisingly small amount

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.820
of light to be highly functional
and yet, very clearly not

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.860
enough for really optimal.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.740
For the people who didn't
like the flashlight,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.740
why did you opt not to use it?

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:08.670
Yeah?

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.030
AUDIENCE: It was just really
harsh compared to-- the candle

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.650
light is a lot softer, and so
when you put the flashlight

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.710
on it it was just this harsh
bright light that it was--

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.140
it couldn't go everywhere
and at one point

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.390
we had some person holding it.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:28.475
It was just, for lack of a
better word, inconvenient.

00:06:28.475 --> 00:06:29.340
AMY BANZAERT: Mhm.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:29.853
Yeah.

00:06:29.853 --> 00:06:31.270
AUDIENCE: Your
eyes adjusted to it

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.320
so that you couldn't
then read anything that

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:35.560
didn't have that light on it.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:36.310
AMY BANZAERT: Mhm.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.235
So then you were beholden to it.

00:06:38.235 --> 00:06:38.860
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:39.652
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:06:39.652 --> 00:06:43.000
And how about the back group?

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.910
AUDIENCE: Well,
we didn't use it.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.160
I started using it
pointing it papers,

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.500
but then my hand kept moving
because I had to squeeze it.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.700
So I think it would be
useful for, I don't know,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.885
like late at night
walking somewhere and just

00:06:55.885 --> 00:06:57.510
shining in front of
you where you don't

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.478
have to focus it on an area.

00:06:59.478 --> 00:07:00.270
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.280
AUDIENCE: But for
the purposes of this,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.420
it was really annoying.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:04.920
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.300
And then your lanterns.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.220
What did you guys notice
about your lanterns?

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.640
Any ones that you were
pleasantly surprised

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.210
or unpleasantly surprised about
the utility of your lantern?

00:07:21.324 --> 00:07:23.920
Oh, a lot of people
notice Carter's reflection

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:24.790
on the ceiling.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.289
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:28.164
AUDIENCE: Aesthetics.

00:07:28.164 --> 00:07:30.235
Just aesthetics.

00:07:30.235 --> 00:07:31.360
AUDIENCE: The real purpose.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.017
AUDIENCE: It was better
sideways than straight up.

00:07:38.017 --> 00:07:39.600
AMY BANZAERT: And
your original intent

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.040
was for it to be straight up?

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:41.340
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:42.132
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:07:42.132 --> 00:07:44.520
I mean, this lights around,
but if you're trying to read

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:45.080
you want it--

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:45.520
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:46.020
Yeah.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.270
How many of you used
your lanterns in the dark

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:49.320
when you built them?

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.410
Cool.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.500
So that's great.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.630
Definitely this is
the kind of thing

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:01.530
where ideally you would
prototype it in the dark

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.952
as you were building it.

00:08:02.952 --> 00:08:04.410
Any other thoughts
on the lanterns?

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.010
OK.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:10.510
Great.

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:16.450
So I want to just run
through any big questions

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:17.710
you guys had on the quiz.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:23.800
And I am going to turn on a
bit more light because it's

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:25.828
really hard to lecture.

00:08:25.828 --> 00:08:27.370
I learned this the
hard way last year

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:31.270
when I couldn't do
anything trying to lecture,

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.010
so I'm going to
get a little more.

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:41.604
Are there any major
questions on the first page?

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:47.300
OK.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.420
And the answers will
be posted on Stellar

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:54.160
so, yeah, you can see them.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:59.189
What did people get on
the first estimation?

00:08:59.189 --> 00:09:00.106
AUDIENCE: What's the--

00:09:00.106 --> 00:09:00.982
AUDIENCE: We don't know what--

00:09:00.982 --> 00:09:02.300
AUDIENCE: We turned
our quizzes in.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.432
AMY BANZAERT: Say again?

00:09:03.432 --> 00:09:03.830
AUDIENCE: Oh, we
turned our quizzes in.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:05.800
AUDIENCE: What was the question?

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:07.480
AMY BANZAERT: That's
really unhelpful.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.120
I'm sorry.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.840
I didn't think this through.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.800
So the first question is, how
many average incandescent light

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.710
bulbs can an average adult
power by pedaling on a bike?

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:19.283
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:09:19.283 --> 00:09:19.726
AUDIENCE: Two.

00:09:19.726 --> 00:09:20.170
AUDIENCE: I put two.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:20.880
AUDIENCE: Two.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.840
AMY BANZAERT: Two, eight.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:23.710
Anywhere else?

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:25.045
AUDIENCE: I have no idea.

00:09:25.045 --> 00:09:26.920
AMY BANZAERT: OK, so
that's about what I got,

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.870
so we're all in the same realm.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:35.350
So 100 to 200 watts pedaling,
which we talked about

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:38.635
in an earlier class, and then--

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.010
excuse me.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:51.910
And then we have 40
to 100 watt bulb.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:58.720
So dividing that out is anywhere
between one and five bulbs.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.405
Is that vaguely
what you guys did?

00:10:00.405 --> 00:10:01.030
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:01.863
AMY BANZAERT: Great.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:06.705
Can you see this
at all in the back?

00:10:06.705 --> 00:10:07.330
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:09.052
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:09.052 --> 00:10:10.510
AMY BANZAERT: So
you no longer have

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:14.630
to maintain your family bonds
so if you want to move forward

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.620
so you can see,
feel free to do so

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.370
because I don't know how to--
maybe I can try this headlamp.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.405
Let me try this one first.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.720
Does that help?

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.660
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.160
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.680
And now I feel really
awesome, fashion-wise.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.180
OK.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:47.300
So second question is, how
long will a solar lantern

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:52.250
last at night turned to its
maximum settings for LEDs?

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:55.460
And so I heard a lot of
questions on this one like,

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.010
what is a solar lantern?

00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.690
I don't have any
context for this.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.140
What are you talking about?

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.380
And one of the muddy
cards that I got last week

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.388
was, why are we doing all
these ridiculous estimations?

00:11:06.388 --> 00:11:08.180
Are you just trying to
make me feel stupid?

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.700
And no, I'm really not trying
to make you feel stupid.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:16.760
And I totally get that when you
get this estimation question

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:22.730
and it's written just the same
way as a normal problem set

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:26.660
problem that is basically like,
how do you integrate x squared?

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:28.550
And you're like, I know
how to do x squared.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:29.993
I know the exact right answer.

00:11:29.993 --> 00:11:31.160
I can do it a hundred times.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.250
And I'm giving you this question
with not enough information,

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:35.510
not enough details.

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:37.400
It's a really different
feeling, and it

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.980
can make you feel really
insecure and foolish

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:41.720
and stressed out.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.690
And part of the reason we're
doing this over and over again

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:47.780
in every class is so you
get used to it and you get

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:50.270
acclimated to it so that
immediate stress response--

