00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.200
so that I can come up
with some of answer.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.790
And that I'm going
to be confident

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:15.920
that I'm not going to
get the right answer,

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:18.253
but I'll probably get it in
the right order of magnitude

00:12:18.253 --> 00:12:19.727
or in the ballpark,
and that that's

00:12:19.727 --> 00:12:20.810
what we're going for here.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:24.120
So it's to get practice
with doing this

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:26.400
so that you start to feel
more comfortable with it.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:28.863
It's never going to feel
the same as when I ask you

00:12:28.863 --> 00:12:30.530
how to integrate x
squared, because it's

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:33.110
the difference between
rote memorization

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:38.300
slash truly understanding
calculus, and approaching

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.560
a brand new problem with
very little information

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.900
and very little time and
knowing how to handle it.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.350
But the latter is
really powerful.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:49.340
Basically anyone can learn
calculus with enough time,

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.850
but learning estimation
takes some practice

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.600
at a different level
that's really, really

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:55.530
valuable long-term.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:59.030
So no, I'm not trying to
make you feel stressed.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:02.810
And the goal is, when
you see this problem,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:07.073
to spend less time on
what the heck and more

00:13:07.073 --> 00:13:08.990
