00:25:12.556 --> 00:25:13.780
AUDIENCE: Quick question.

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:15.547
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah?

00:25:15.547 --> 00:25:17.005
AUDIENCE: Is it
kerosene power then

00:25:17.005 --> 00:25:20.320
or whatever that we use when
we're outside lanterns to keep

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:21.728
the mosquitoes away sometimes?

00:25:21.728 --> 00:25:23.020
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so that's--

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:23.080
AUDIENCE: You know
what I'm talking about?

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:24.460
Kind of like luau torches.

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:24.870
AUDIENCE: Tiki torches.

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:25.205
AMY BANZAERT: Mhm.

00:25:25.205 --> 00:25:25.540
Yeah.

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:26.590
It's the same kerosene.

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:31.240
So that's one of the
things that kerosene offers

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:32.748
is mosquito repelling.

00:25:32.748 --> 00:25:34.540
And that's one of the
things to think about

00:25:34.540 --> 00:25:38.230
for all of these lights is that
traditional lights do offer

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.740
some benefits that people
like, like mosquito repelling,

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:45.550
like heating in some areas.

00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:49.160
And just going back to grid
light bulbs for a minute,

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:50.750
we have a setup here.

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:53.570
So watch your eyes.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:58.610
This is a 40 watt
incandescent light bulb,

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:02.898
which is comically
low in normal housing,

00:26:02.898 --> 00:26:04.690
and yet, when we're
used to this low light,

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:06.470
is ridiculously painful.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:10.840
So then you can add a reflector,
which makes it a little less

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:13.400
miserable to use.

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.780
But if you notice just
the color of the light

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:20.920
and then compare it
to this incandescent--

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:24.788
or this fluorescent light, this
isn't the fluorescent light

00:26:24.788 --> 00:26:26.830
that's the same color as
these fluorescent bulbs.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:30.790
They've actually done a lot to
improve fluorescent light color

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:32.950
temperature to make it
more pleasing to the eye,

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:37.780
whereas LED lights are still
quite comparatively blue.

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:40.030
This one has a nice diffuser
so it's less painful.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:42.670
But you can see how
drastically different the color

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:45.340
of the light is, and
light color really

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.975
tends to impact people's
mood and perception of--

00:26:48.975 --> 00:26:49.600
AUDIENCE: Life.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:52.142
AMY BANZAERT: --the life and
how much they like the lighting.

00:26:52.142 --> 00:26:55.120
So while LEDs have
a lot of potential,

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:57.670
the color temperature
issue is strong.

00:27:01.291 --> 00:27:04.750
The light I was using
earlier, the headlamp,

00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:09.770
was really annoying because
it's great for task lighting.

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:11.500
This is a better one, actually.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:14.620
But it's very-- blind
you really fast, right?

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:18.910
So they invented
this diffuser, which

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:24.250
I think ups the dork
factor an amazing amount.

00:27:24.250 --> 00:27:25.570
And there's a reason.

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:28.690
I found this at
IRE on more than--

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:32.050
it was, like, 75% off because
I think no one's buying this.

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:36.230
Because while the idea of a
diffuser makes a lot of sense,

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:39.070
if it doesn't look
good, you're in trouble,

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:43.540
and this really has a lot to
be desired in terms of how good

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:44.830
it looks on.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:48.460
And that's true
everywhere, that what

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.915
a device looks like matters.

00:27:50.915 --> 00:27:51.415
OK.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.013
So yeah, so at the break we
can look through all of these.

00:27:58.013 --> 00:28:00.180
You can look through all
of these different lanterns

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:01.140
we have.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.720
OK, so I'm going to turn
on the projector now.

00:28:07.910 --> 00:28:10.550
About the Kinkajou
projector, which

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:22.840
was actually originally
invented here at MIT in 2009.

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:31.150
So here we-- so that's
the Kinkajou, which,

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:35.380
as I mentioned, is an offshoot.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:36.850
And you can notice
already how much

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:40.030
brighter this classroom is
just from the little projector.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:42.910
It's shocking from
our projector,

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:45.400
which is amazingly bright now.

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:47.500
So the Kinkajou
projector, again,

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:50.110
came out of MIT's 2009
class and then went

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.160
through a lot of iterations
to where it is now.

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:54.290
So I wanted to show some
of those iterations.

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:56.080
So this was the
initial prototype

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:58.900
that came out of 2009.

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:02.980
I need to pointer or something.

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:06.400
There, and you can see
it being tested here.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.650
And then it went
through two iterations

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.305
to where it currently is today.

00:29:10.305 --> 00:29:11.680
You might have
heard them mention

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:14.380
that they used
Fisher-Price optics, which

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:16.570
sounds really funny, right?

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:20.020
But toy companies
do an amazing job

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.960
of getting costs down really
low and then producing

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:23.930
in high volume.

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:26.867
And so one of the things
that can be really handy

00:29:26.867 --> 00:29:28.450
is if you know that
their toy is going

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.430
to stay in production
for a long time,

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:32.170
you can harvest those parts.

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.713
And that's something D-Lab
Health has been starting to do.

00:29:34.713 --> 00:29:36.130
If you know that
something is just

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:38.505
going to be readily available
for the foreseeable future,

00:29:38.505 --> 00:29:40.330
why not use those parts?

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.750
It's a lot cheaper than
manufacturing yourself or even

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:46.060
buying it yourself sometimes.

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:50.380
So the reason I wanted to show
this is just because I think

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:51.880
a lot of-- one of
the reasons I want

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:53.463
to show this is
because a lot of times

00:29:53.463 --> 00:29:56.860
we think about prototypes as
like, oh, we have the prototype

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:58.150
and then we have production.

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:01.330
And instead, in 2009
they probably did

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:03.520
three or four prototypes
to get to this point.

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:05.320
And then this just
shows three prototypes,

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:07.570
and there's actually
many more that they

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:10.090
had to get through
to get to a version

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:12.828
that they could actually
produce in relative quantities.

00:30:12.828 --> 00:30:14.620
And this is still in
the order of magnitude

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:17.980
of hundreds of projectors,
not thousands or tens

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:20.050
of thousands or millions.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:21.700
One aspect of the
Kinkajou projector

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:24.580
that also came up in
2009 in its second year

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:27.670
was, how do you
power the Kinkajou?

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:32.440
And so students worked on this
device, which is basically

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:35.710
a rowing machine, to power the
device, which worked really

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:36.790
well.

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:39.550
Harvard students came up with
a treadle powered device.

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:41.290
And then what they're
using currently

00:30:41.290 --> 00:30:45.550
is a battery pack that
connects to a solar panel.

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:48.880
These devices, can you think
why a solar panel might

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:50.923
be chosen over these devices?

00:30:50.923 --> 00:30:52.090
AUDIENCE: Way more portable.

00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:53.423
AMY BANZAERT: Way more portable.

00:30:53.423 --> 00:30:54.137
Absolutely.

00:30:54.137 --> 00:30:55.095
AUDIENCE: Minimal work.

00:30:55.095 --> 00:30:56.220
AMY BANZAERT: Minimal work.

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:57.030
Yeah.

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:57.887
AUDIENCE: Noise.

00:30:57.887 --> 00:30:58.720
AMY BANZAERT: Noise.

00:30:58.720 --> 00:30:59.980
Yup.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:00.820
AUDIENCE: Timesaver.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:01.750
AMY BANZAERT: Timesaver.

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:02.250
Yeah.

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:07.720
So the price of these two
sets is relatively similar,

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:10.780
but it's a whole lot easier for
Kinkajou to ship solar panels

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.400
than it is to ship
stuff like this.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.000
And then there are
some gender issues

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:18.040
which were addressed
between this and this

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:21.190
in terms of who's
allowed to use the power,

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:27.790
but still, no one wants to
be sitting riding a bike

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:32.830
or doing a rowing machine
while you're in the classroom

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:35.560
if instead you could
have a solar panel.

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.380
So while human power is great,
and we talked about that a lot

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:45.660
last time, there's still
opportunity for improvement.

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:46.800
OK.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:52.380
So this lecture is adapted
from Suzanne Seitinger, who

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:54.930
was in the Smart Cities
part of the Media Lab

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:56.880
and she got her PhD last year.

00:31:56.880 --> 00:32:00.280
I'm not sure where she is now.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:01.990
But she taught this
lecture last year,

00:32:01.990 --> 00:32:05.140
so that's where
this is coming from.

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:08.190
So just to give a
little bit of context,

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:11.880
1 and 1/2 billion people don't
have access to electricity.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:14.820
About a third of the
population in this situation

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:16.492
lives in India, so
that's why a lot

00:32:16.492 --> 00:32:18.450
of the projects we're
going to be talking about

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:19.950
are based in India.

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:26.190
Also India has a relatively high
growth that's providing wealth

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:29.280
to a lot of people, and
then still a very second set

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.360
of people who are getting
very little benefit.

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.720
And so the contrast
means there's

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.310
some resources and
some opportunity

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:37.530
there to do work there.

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:39.180
And the International
Energy Agency

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:42.550
doesn't predict this is
going to change very quickly.

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:47.070
So most people are relying on
other fuels, kerosene, dung,

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:50.370
wood, diesel, candles,
battery powered flashlights,

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:52.170
for lighting.

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:54.930
And all of those have
some real drawbacks.

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:58.110
You saw the drawbacks
of kerosene and candles,

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.600
at least some of them.

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.690
You can imagine the drawback
of battery powered flashlights.

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:07.110
If nothing else, it's just
the cost of those batteries.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.000
Wood is really dirty.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:12.360
Dung is as dirty as it gets in
terms of indoor air pollution

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:13.830
issues.

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:16.140
And it also interestingly,
just as wood

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:18.870
is bad for the environment, dung
is also bad for the environment

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:20.580
because it's a really
great fertilizer.

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:25.000
And in regions where they use a
lot of dung to do other things,

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:28.510
the quality of the soil
goes down dramatically.

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:31.200
And then diesel, unsurprisingly,
is rather dirty and dangerous,

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:33.660
similar to kerosene.

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:36.300
One other issue that's
really interesting

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:45.370
is how much you pay if you
have modern lighting as opposed

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:46.270
to kerosene.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:49.190
It gets very
expensive very fast.

00:33:49.190 --> 00:33:51.790
So this is just a
set of the range

00:33:51.790 --> 00:33:55.960
of how expensive
different lighting is

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:59.920
compared to how light it is.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:03.520
And this slide is
really hard to parse,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.160
and I added color to it to make
it a little easier to parse.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:09.730
But basically what it's
showing is how much--

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:15.100
the y-intercept is how
much the initial cost is

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:17.860
and then the slope is what
is the continuing cost.

00:34:17.860 --> 00:34:22.120
So ideally you want to be
right down there, right?

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:26.350
So free to start and as low
as possible-- a very low slope

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:28.510
so that the cost of
ownership isn't going up.

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:37.150
So for example, a solar 5 watt
compact fluorescent lantern

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:42.032
is really expensive and it
just keeps going up in cost

00:34:42.032 --> 00:34:44.199
because even though you
think it's solar, it's free,

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:47.929
it tends to break a lot, and
so the cost keeps going up

00:34:47.929 --> 00:34:50.170
so it's really expensive.

00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:54.100
Candles start out free,
but they keep going up.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:55.929
One thing I found
really interesting

00:34:55.929 --> 00:35:02.050
was a simple kerosene lamp has
a pretty nice low slope compared

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:03.620
to most of these technologies.

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.400
And then the hurricane
kerosene lamp goes way up,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:08.150
and I was wondering
what's the difference.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:10.570
Does anyone have a thought?

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:12.490
AUDIENCE: What is a
hurricane kerosene lamp?

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:14.530
AMY BANZAERT: So a
simple kerosene lamp

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:18.850
is like this one and then
a hurricane kerosene lamp--

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:21.970
it's like the classic
lantern that you

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:26.500
imagine, sort of like this,
but with kerosene in it

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:28.240
instead of--

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:30.910
any thoughts as to what the
difference, the discrepancy is?

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:35.010
Yeah?

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:37.052
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't the
glass have to be replaced?

00:35:37.052 --> 00:35:40.680
AMY BANZAERT: So that's
one possibility, yeah.

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:43.380
AUDIENCE: Does it burn brighter?

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:46.110
AMY BANZAERT: I don't
think it burns brighter.

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:46.815
Yeah?

00:35:46.815 --> 00:35:48.482
AUDIENCE: Doesn't it
come with something

00:35:48.482 --> 00:35:50.918
on the inside that
has to be replaced?

00:35:50.918 --> 00:35:51.710
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:52.210
Yeah.

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:55.230
So both lanterns
have wicks, right?

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:57.990
That's how you get the
kerosene lantern to work.

00:35:57.990 --> 00:36:01.630
And a simple kerosene
lamp can use really,

00:36:01.630 --> 00:36:03.540
really simple wick
and a hurricane lamp

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:06.490
requires more expensive wick,
and so that's the cost driver.

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:08.110
And it's phenomenal
because you think,

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:10.805
oh, I'd so much prefer
a hurricane lamp

00:36:10.805 --> 00:36:12.180
because it's going
to enclose it,

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:13.980
it's going to make it
a little less smoky,

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:16.292
it's going to make the
candles flicker a little less.

00:36:16.292 --> 00:36:18.000
But if you have to
use this special wick,

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:21.990
it's going to cost so
much more very quickly.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:24.570
And then a grid connected
60 watt incandescent

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:27.210
lamp is pretty great.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:29.670
A compact fluorescent, as
we're all familiar with,

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:32.520
cost a little more initially,
but then your slope improves

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:36.520
and hopefully you make back
your purchase pretty quickly.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:39.503
So I found this graph to
be really interesting.

00:36:39.503 --> 00:36:40.920
When you started
to have questions

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.110
about what the
differences are, I put up

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:46.830
the assumptions for that table.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:50.100
But generally up to 30% of--

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:51.030
yeah, Sanon?

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:53.688
AUDIENCE: I just had
a question about CFM.

00:36:53.688 --> 00:36:54.480
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah?

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:56.250
AUDIENCE: So when you
talk about the slope

00:36:56.250 --> 00:36:58.740
as compared to kerosene,
are you talking about--

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:01.680
like on what basis
are you estimating

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:03.060
the cost of electricity?

00:37:03.060 --> 00:37:05.197
Is that the cost in
the US or the cost in--

00:37:05.197 --> 00:37:06.780
AMY BANZAERT: So you
can see on here--

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:09.238
I mean, you can't read it right
now because it's too small,

00:37:09.238 --> 00:37:11.070
but you can see the assumptions.

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:15.900
So they assumed that grid
electricity in rural areas is

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:17.725
about $0.10 per kilowatt hour.

00:37:17.725 --> 00:37:19.350
AUDIENCE: Just kidding,
I couldn't see.

00:37:19.350 --> 00:37:20.220
AMY BANZAERT: No, of
course, you can't see that.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.330
That's impossible.

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:22.490
I can barely read it.

00:37:22.490 --> 00:37:24.990
So I'm not-- I didn't put this
up so that you could read it,

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:26.440
but that you have
it on your slides

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:28.273
so that you can look
through it later if you

00:37:28.273 --> 00:37:29.920
have questions like that.

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:30.420
Yeah.

00:37:30.420 --> 00:37:32.170
And I didn't expect
you to be able to read

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:35.220
that whole slide in all of
three seconds or whatever.

00:37:35.220 --> 00:37:37.297
So yeah, but I put
that up there so when

00:37:37.297 --> 00:37:38.880
you have these
questions of comparison

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:40.830
it's easy to figure out
what the assumptions were.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.580
And these assumptions
are a few years old.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:45.540
So some of the drawbacks
of solar and LED lighting

00:37:45.540 --> 00:37:48.480
are getting better,
but there's still--

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:51.450
I'd still say we're in
relatively the same picture

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:52.990
with some improvement.

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:55.320
So financial costs are a
big issue with lighting.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:59.160
So people can spend up to
30% of their income on light.

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:04.705
And in this country,
3% is the best estimate

00:38:04.705 --> 00:38:05.580
I could come up with.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:08.580
But it's really, really
high because when

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:12.487
we collect data on what
we spend in the US,

00:38:12.487 --> 00:38:14.820
you don't really break it
out-- like you don't break out

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:16.740
utilities and how much
of your electric bill

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:18.810
is light versus how much
is your electric stove

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:21.810
versus your electric
water heater, et cetera.

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:23.830
So 3% is really high.

00:38:23.830 --> 00:38:26.070
So people can be spending
up to 10 times more

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:28.290
than we're used
to spending, which

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:31.800
has a huge burden, of course.

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:36.000
Environmental costs are severe
from the proper disposal

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.615
of batteries to
wood deforestation.

00:38:39.615 --> 00:38:41.490
And then one that we
don't always think about

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:42.730
is light pollution.

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:46.380
So that's less of an issue
in the super rural areas,

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:49.335
but it is affecting
everywhere more and more,

00:38:49.335 --> 00:38:51.210
and certainly in this
country light pollution

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:53.220
has a really big impact.

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:55.450
It's great to have light
from a safety perspective,

00:38:55.450 --> 00:38:58.860
but then from other
perspectives light pollution

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:01.763
can be really problematic in
terms of pleasure at night

00:39:01.763 --> 00:39:03.180
from being able
to enjoy the night

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:05.560
sky, being able to have
a darkened room at night,

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:06.060
et cetera.

00:39:08.830 --> 00:39:11.250
Social costs of
not having light.

00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:14.190
So everything becomes
difficult at night,

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:17.520
as I think became
clear just now.

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:18.990
So people who
don't have light go

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:23.570
to bed really early compared
to people who do have light.

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:26.220
There's some agricultural
work and other type of work

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:28.200
that need to happen
at night, either

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:30.900
because they have peak
periods where like

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:32.820
if you don't fish in these--

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:35.700
this one month you want to fish
as much as humanly possible,

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.280
and if you can't fish at
night you lose a ton of money,

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:42.030
to things like people
collecting rose petals.

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:45.720
That has to be done at night
because once the sun comes up,

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:50.070
the flowers start
to wilt. It means

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:51.690
you just have less
time in your day

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:54.600
because you only have
the time of the sun.

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:58.080
It disrupts education if you
can't do any night school.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.550
You can imagine
how challenging it

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:01.980
is to do surgery
with no light and get

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:03.120
medical care in no light.

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:04.620
And so if you have
any problems when

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:07.300
the sun isn't up medically,
that can be really dangerous.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:09.850
Then of course, just
security issues.

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:14.670
It's well understood that having
lighting helps with security.

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:18.300
In terms of health costs,
breathing kerosene fumes

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:21.480
and other smoke in
indoor places is awful,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:26.370
including the particulate
method emissions.

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:28.620
There's just the
danger of fire started

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:31.200
when you have a fire lit.

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.360
That's why I actually
included the candle

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:36.270
holders for the first candle,
is it gets-- those candles get

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:38.700
really hot and I didn't
want anyone to get burned.

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:44.100
And then just eyestrain
makes your days harder.

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:47.250
So I think now is a good
time to take a break

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.820
and then after the
break, we'll talk

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:53.180
about all those different
measurements of lux

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:55.200
and lumens and all
that good stuff.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:59.610
So as I mentioned earlier,
it's a dynamic process.

00:40:59.610 --> 00:41:01.910
It's contingent on
a lot of variables,

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:05.780
like what are the
requirements of the task.

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:09.590
You can imagine that reading
is a very different task

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:12.260
than cooking in terms of what
lighting requirements you need,

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:16.430
but both you need significant
amounts of light to do well.

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:18.500
Mood and atmosphere
are important,

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:23.210
the appearance, the aesthetics,
the visual comfort related,

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:27.410
health, safety, well being,
the social communication

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:30.560
aspect of lighting, the
different people's point

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:33.750
of view, costs and
environmental concerns.

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:36.330
So there's a lot to consider.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:37.790
And some specifics.

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:42.470
So brightness is
both the intensity

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:43.640
and the amount of light.

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:48.800
And you can see the extremes
of staring at the sun,

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.500
thankfully through a
camera lens, and how bright

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.460
that is versus the glows.

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.310
And this is not coming
out that well so I'm

00:41:55.310 --> 00:41:57.102
going to turn down the
lights a little bit.

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:02.570
Does that look a little better?

00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:03.680
Or the same?

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:04.370
I can't tell.

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:07.370
It looks a little
better to my eye.

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:10.190
So the glow, and then
glare is an issue

00:42:10.190 --> 00:42:11.960
that you guys all
experienced, I think,

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:14.930
today of getting shined
at by one of my lights

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:16.820
or one of your
lights in your face

00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:19.460
and you really can't see a thing
except for the light that's

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:20.600
blinding your eyes.

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:25.880
So I won't glare--
blind you right now.

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:28.230
Contrast is another
thing to consider.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:31.550
So something with
very positive contrast

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:34.760
where the object is
brighter than the background

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:37.610
as opposed to the reverse
where all you can see

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:41.790
is the silhouette
because the background is

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:45.690
brighter than the foreground.

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.600
And again, obviously
if there's no contrast

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:50.260
you can't see anything.

00:42:50.260 --> 00:42:51.930
And then one thing
to think about

00:42:51.930 --> 00:42:55.060
is illusions associated
with contrast.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:57.060
So you've probably
seen this before,

00:42:57.060 --> 00:43:00.700
but this color gray and this
color gray are the same color

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:02.580
and it's just the
black versus the white

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:05.880
that changes your
perception of gray.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:09.870
You can click here in the slides
to go see a cool animation

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:14.160
where they actually move the
gray off and on so you can

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:15.960
literally watch
the contrast happen

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:17.980
and the perceived
change in the gray color

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:19.230
happen right before your eyes.

00:43:19.230 --> 00:43:20.487
It's kind of cool.

00:43:20.487 --> 00:43:21.945
And that's called
White's illusion.

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:30.810
You guys all experienced candles
in terms of contrast issues.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:34.770
And you'll notice,
obviously I'm not going to--

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:37.626
I could light a candle
right now, so why don't I?

00:43:37.626 --> 00:43:41.230
When it was pitch dark,
the candle was really,

00:43:41.230 --> 00:43:42.945
really evident and useful.

00:43:48.260 --> 00:43:53.140
And now it's kind of bright,
but it's not as shocking

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:55.750
or as useful as it was.

00:43:55.750 --> 00:43:58.780
And then another thing to
notice in this picture--

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.780
and because it's a painting
it's a whole lot easier to get

00:44:01.780 --> 00:44:04.570
the contrast than if it
were a camera, which is why

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:05.890
it's a nice picture to use--

00:44:05.890 --> 00:44:07.960
is that this candlelight
works pretty well

00:44:07.960 --> 00:44:10.270
for lighting her face,
but it's probably

00:44:10.270 --> 00:44:13.060
pretty terrible for
lighting her way in terms

00:44:13.060 --> 00:44:14.750
of not tripping on anything.

00:44:14.750 --> 00:44:17.950
And so candles have some
aspects that they work really

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:21.700
well on and others not so much.

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:23.750
The color temperature
issue that we talked about.

00:44:23.750 --> 00:44:27.520
So I think this will
be less painful now.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:30.500
Still a little painful,
but not as bad.

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:33.310
So the extremes.

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:37.720
And you can see the candle color
temperature versus incandescent

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.690
versus fluorescent versus
like improved fluorescent

00:44:40.690 --> 00:44:44.020
versus a more traditional
fluorescent versus the LED.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.460
It's a huge range.

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:49.390
And even if we had no lighting
and just were using the sun,

00:44:49.390 --> 00:44:53.350
the difference between
sunlight and shade

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:54.710
is pretty significant.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:57.280
So we're used to some
range, so expecting

00:44:57.280 --> 00:45:00.700
that there's some perfect
color that is the one we're

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:02.710
shooting for is not necessary.

00:45:02.710 --> 00:45:06.730
But candles certainly are on
the far end of the spectrum.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:10.540
And LEDs aren't shown here,
but are on the opposite end

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:11.678
and pretty unpleasant.

00:45:11.678 --> 00:45:13.220
AUDIENCE: What's
the unit of measure?

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:13.958
Is that correct?

00:45:13.958 --> 00:45:15.500
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
so that's Kelvin,

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:18.610
so it's a heat temperature.

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:20.398
Measuring all of
these-- you can read up

00:45:20.398 --> 00:45:21.940
on all the different
ways that people

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:25.107
measure this sort of
thing and the controversy

00:45:25.107 --> 00:45:26.440
over the different measurements.

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:27.880
Again, because
it's so subjective

00:45:27.880 --> 00:45:31.600
it's really hard to get a really
analytical measurement that

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:36.040
captures all aspects
of light performance.

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:40.190
So speaking of that, just
going through the terms.

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:45.160
So the lumen-- and I find this
diagram to be really helpful--

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:48.790
is the light that's
being emitted.

00:45:48.790 --> 00:45:51.370
And then the luminous
intensity or candela

00:45:51.370 --> 00:45:55.040
is the lumens per angle.

00:45:55.040 --> 00:45:59.740
And then illuminance or lux is
the lumens per square meter.

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:04.900
And then luminance is
the luminous intensity

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:05.853
that reaches the eye.

00:46:05.853 --> 00:46:07.270
Is everyone totally
clear on that?

00:46:07.270 --> 00:46:08.920
Yeah.

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.920
So yeah, all the words
are somewhat similar

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:15.232
and we're just talking
about different ways

00:46:15.232 --> 00:46:15.940
of looking at it.

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:18.640
And because you can only ever
see the light using your eye,

00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:20.530
unless you have a light meter--

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:23.290
which we'll be getting but
we don't have right now--

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:27.340
it's difficult to even conceive
of exactly what these mean

00:46:27.340 --> 00:46:29.800
apart from just hypothetically.

00:46:29.800 --> 00:46:33.490
And then, of course, brightness
is subjective and not something

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:36.750
that's really easy to measure.

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:37.860
You guys look confused.

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:38.778
Do you look confused--

00:46:38.778 --> 00:46:40.778
AUDIENCE: Well, I feel
like when I think of flux

00:46:40.778 --> 00:46:43.400
I think of something
going over a certain are.

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:46.010
But it's just [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:50.480
So I understand lumen as an
energy and the amount of light

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:55.140
that's emitted,
but not as a flux.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:59.503
I feel like luminous flux
would be more of a candela.

00:46:59.503 --> 00:47:00.920
AMY BANZAERT: So
I understand what

00:47:00.920 --> 00:47:02.630
you're saying, that
the way flux is

00:47:02.630 --> 00:47:05.990
used in this set of definitions
is contrary to the way you're

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:08.030
used to seeing flux.

00:47:08.030 --> 00:47:12.380
And I cannot take credit
for these definitions so I

00:47:12.380 --> 00:47:14.420
can't help you
resolve that conflict,

00:47:14.420 --> 00:47:17.030
only to agree with
that it is confusing.

00:47:22.376 --> 00:47:24.648
AUDIENCE: Does that
illuminance versus luminance?

00:47:24.648 --> 00:47:25.690
AMY BANZAERT: You got it.

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.270
AUDIENCE: What's a steradian?

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:31.270
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:34.900
AUDIENCE: It says under luminous
intensity, candela or luminance

00:47:34.900 --> 00:47:36.810
per steradian.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:38.130
AMY BANZAERT: Per radian?

00:47:38.130 --> 00:47:38.940
Steradian.

00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:46.650
So a steradian is, again, a
strange measure of an angle.

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:52.290
I'm not going to ask you
to derive or calculate

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:55.080
luminous intensity,
just so that you're

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:56.580
familiar with these existence.

00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:57.770
Yeah, Sanon?

00:47:57.770 --> 00:48:00.030
AUDIENCE: No, I think the
steradian is a solid angle,

00:48:00.030 --> 00:48:00.300
right?

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:01.220
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:01.450
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:48:01.450 --> 00:48:03.591
No, I was just going to
ask the same question.

00:48:03.591 --> 00:48:04.466
AUDIENCE: What is it?

00:48:04.466 --> 00:48:06.640
AUDIENCE: Steradian's the
measure of a solid angle.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.313
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:48:08.313 --> 00:48:10.480
AUDIENCE: It's like if you
have a circle you already

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:14.290
have a radian, which means it's
just mapping that to a sphere.

00:48:14.290 --> 00:48:16.420
So it's the same
idea as a radian,

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:18.038
except it's a
radian for a sphere.

00:48:18.038 --> 00:48:18.580
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:20.038
That makes sense.

00:48:20.038 --> 00:48:22.470
Thanks a lot.

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:23.640
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:28.800
And so here are a bunch of these
quantities with symbols and SI

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:32.940
units, some of which
help and some of which

00:48:32.940 --> 00:48:34.200
add to the confusion.

00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:36.840
Like the difference
between light incident

00:48:36.840 --> 00:48:39.400
on a surface versus light
emitted from a surface,

00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:41.370
it all gets complicated.

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:44.160
And so basically, the
takeaway from this

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:47.430
is that if you want to actually
compare two lights and say,

00:48:47.430 --> 00:48:50.550
this one is superior to
that one, it's a lot of work

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:51.660
and it's subjective.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:54.750
Even with hypothetically
some of these objective

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:56.910
measures it's
challenging because

00:48:56.910 --> 00:49:00.690
of the nature of our eyes.

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:05.920
Just like odor is
challenging to measure too.

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:10.380
So when you're using a
basic light meter you're

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:18.390
measuring illuminance, which
again, going back here,

00:49:18.390 --> 00:49:21.450
is just measuring the light
emitted onto a surface.

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:28.535
So you can compare when
you-- using a light meter,

00:49:28.535 --> 00:49:29.910
some of the things
you want to do

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:34.170
are compare vertical surfaces
and horizontal surfaces looking

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:36.300
at where the light source is.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:39.840
Usually it's very rare in
a room or in a location

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:42.510
that there's only
one light source.

00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:44.830
When we all had
just four candles

00:49:44.830 --> 00:49:46.080
there were four light sources.

00:49:46.080 --> 00:49:47.580
If only one family
had the candle,

00:49:47.580 --> 00:49:49.080
it would have been
one light source.

00:49:49.080 --> 00:49:50.628
But normally
there's a whole lot.

00:49:50.628 --> 00:49:52.420
There's my computer,
there's the projector,

00:49:52.420 --> 00:49:54.870
there's the overhead
lights, there would be light

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:56.730
coming in from the
windows of the doors

00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:58.020
if I hadn't covered them up.

00:49:58.020 --> 00:50:01.110
So you have to consider
all of those, and then

00:50:01.110 --> 00:50:03.240
the stray light sources.

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:05.220
And then whether you're
measuring at eye level

00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:06.540
and where exactly is eye level.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:08.760
Are you assuming an average
woman or an average man

00:50:08.760 --> 00:50:10.398
or something else.

00:50:10.398 --> 00:50:12.690
And then also at the task
surface as opposed to the eye

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:13.440
surface.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:14.910
All of those are important.

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:21.210
Just to give you a sense of
order of magnitude, of how

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:24.060
bright and how unbright--

00:50:24.060 --> 00:50:26.672
yeah, did you have a question?

00:50:26.672 --> 00:50:29.130
Eric, did you have a question
or was your hand just moving?

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:29.610
AUDIENCE: What?

00:50:29.610 --> 00:50:30.570
I was just moving my hand.

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:31.403
AMY BANZAERT: Sorry.

00:50:31.403 --> 00:50:32.250
OK.

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:35.100
So we can range a
whole lot of order

00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:39.420
of magnitudes from a
bright star at night,

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:41.130
which is still pretty dim.

00:50:41.130 --> 00:50:43.813
But if you've ever been
in the middle of nowhere

00:50:43.813 --> 00:50:45.480
in the middle of the
night, it's amazing

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:48.130
how bright stars
can be on a moon

00:50:48.130 --> 00:50:50.670
with-- in a night with no moon.

00:50:50.670 --> 00:50:54.870
It's surprisingly bright
once your eyes adapt.

00:50:54.870 --> 00:51:04.740
To how strong a moon is to a
living room, hallway toilet

00:51:04.740 --> 00:51:05.612
versus living room.

00:51:05.612 --> 00:51:08.070
This really depends on how well
your toilet and your living

00:51:08.070 --> 00:51:10.390
room are lit compared
to each other.

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:11.740
There's a lot of variation here.

00:51:11.740 --> 00:51:13.380
So this is general
metrics, but there's

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:15.300
a lot of variation, of course.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:18.780
And then sunrise
or sunset is going

00:51:18.780 --> 00:51:21.090
to be much brighter
than your living room.

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:24.120
And then going all the
way to full daylight

00:51:24.120 --> 00:51:28.260
and then direct sunlight is
incredibly bright and piercing.

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:30.690
So if you think about
sitting on a beach

00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:34.020
with no clouds, just
how bright that is,

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:35.670
it's shockingly bright.

00:51:35.670 --> 00:51:36.547
Yeah.

00:51:36.547 --> 00:51:39.250
AUDIENCE: How does an
overcast day have more lux

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:41.877
than a sunrise or a sunset?

00:51:41.877 --> 00:51:43.210
AMY BANZAERT: What do you think?

00:51:47.990 --> 00:51:50.630
Does anyone have a
hypothesis for why

00:51:50.630 --> 00:51:55.940
an overcast day has more
lux than sunrise or sunset?

00:51:55.940 --> 00:52:00.230
AUDIENCE: I guess the overcast
day has clouds as a diffuser

00:52:00.230 --> 00:52:02.570
and then for a sunrise and
sunset it's not direct sun,

00:52:02.570 --> 00:52:07.110
but more of the--

00:52:07.110 --> 00:52:10.540
I don't know which word to use.

00:52:10.540 --> 00:52:11.040
Non-direct.

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:11.873
AMY BANZAERT: Right.

00:52:11.873 --> 00:52:14.490
But it's very indirect
and the angle of the sun

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:17.160
is much lower on the
horizon, so the percentage

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:21.930
of the sun that's actually--
the rays that are actually

00:52:21.930 --> 00:52:25.930
providing light to you
is significantly lower.

00:52:25.930 --> 00:52:28.260
And there's a lot
more diffusion.

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:31.170
Yeah.

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:34.160
So there's recommendations for
task lighting, again, in lux,

00:52:34.160 --> 00:52:36.630
so we can use the light meter.

00:52:36.630 --> 00:52:38.580
And you can see that
they're typically

00:52:38.580 --> 00:52:41.430
measured in horizontal as
well as vertical luminance

00:52:41.430 --> 00:52:44.250
and that a kitchen,
reading, desk

00:52:44.250 --> 00:52:47.050
work all require a
certain level much,

00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:50.790
much higher than if you're
just doing general tasks.

00:52:50.790 --> 00:52:53.790
And you saw that today
that we could get away

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:58.950
with a ridiculously low level
of lux in this room and function

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:01.570
when we just had candles
and maybe a little bit more.

00:53:01.570 --> 00:53:04.080
But if you wanted to be
really reading or studying

00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:07.598
comfortably, you wanted
tremendous amount more lux.

00:53:07.598 --> 00:53:09.390
AUDIENCE: What do they
mean by circulation?

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:11.670
Just that going about
your daily activities.

00:53:11.670 --> 00:53:13.510
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:53:13.510 --> 00:53:14.580
AUDIENCE: Quick question.

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:17.790
Vertical luminance would be from
the top to the bottom, right?

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:19.415
Not like going down vertically.

00:53:19.415 --> 00:53:21.540
AMY BANZAERT: So they're
talking about measuring it

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:23.914
on a horizontal surface
and a vertical surface.

00:53:23.914 --> 00:53:26.081
AUDIENCE: OK, so incident
on a vertical and incident

00:53:26.081 --> 00:53:29.320
on a horizontal surface.

00:53:29.320 --> 00:53:31.900
AMY BANZAERT: And
then also outside we

00:53:31.900 --> 00:53:34.210
have to worry about a
lot of different ranges.

00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:40.210
And you can see that you care
a whole lot more about lighting

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:43.750
a monument if you care about
everyone seeing the monument

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:46.570
then you do, say,
in your garden.

00:53:46.570 --> 00:53:51.490
It can be a whole lot dimmer and
people can still walk around.

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:54.070
And again, when I was talking
about all the different areas

00:53:54.070 --> 00:53:58.420
that you have to worry
about, you have glare.

00:53:58.420 --> 00:53:59.680
This is the point of interest.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:01.690
You have light
pollution that's going

00:54:01.690 --> 00:54:04.390
to affect your measurements.

00:54:04.390 --> 00:54:07.510
You have some
reflective surfaces.

00:54:07.510 --> 00:54:10.240
All of these different areas
are things to consider.

00:54:10.240 --> 00:54:13.870
This is one way of looking at
it with a physical diagram,

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:17.140
and this is another one that
runs through the different ways

00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:21.130
that you can measure these
different light issues.

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:24.550
OK, so I've gotten through
all these different basics

00:54:24.550 --> 00:54:28.150
on how to measure light
and these basic criteria.

00:54:28.150 --> 00:54:31.000
There's a whole lot more
content that we could go through

00:54:31.000 --> 00:54:33.880
that I'm not an expert in
and I don't think is crucial.

00:54:33.880 --> 00:54:35.440
But if you do get
into this area,

00:54:35.440 --> 00:54:39.020
it's deep and interesting.

00:54:39.020 --> 00:54:42.100
So again, we talked
about just the quality

00:54:42.100 --> 00:54:44.350
of the light of
a hurricane lamp,

00:54:44.350 --> 00:54:47.650
say, lit by kerosene
versus an LED light.

00:54:47.650 --> 00:54:51.700
The experience is
very, very different

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:54.070
both in terms of the
experience of how bright it

00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:58.430
is, the color of the light,
the cleanliness, the cost.

00:54:58.430 --> 00:55:01.790
All of these things
are quite distinct.

00:55:01.790 --> 00:55:06.265
And this graph or
this chart really

00:55:06.265 --> 00:55:08.090
tries to document
those differences.

00:55:08.090 --> 00:55:13.060
So the brightness is
pretty severely different.

00:55:13.060 --> 00:55:15.670
A fuel based light is
much, much less bright

00:55:15.670 --> 00:55:17.260
typically than electrical light.

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:19.870
The quality is much
poorer, which you guys

00:55:19.870 --> 00:55:24.160
saw, and then the reliability--

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.560
well, either one can
be unreliable, right?

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:31.340
And you have to define what
you mean by reliability,

00:55:31.340 --> 00:55:33.190
what's more important to you.

00:55:33.190 --> 00:55:35.140
Relying on the grid is
great until the grid

00:55:35.140 --> 00:55:39.412
becomes so unreliable that
you stop relying on it.

00:55:39.412 --> 00:55:40.870
Which has happened
to my neighbors,

00:55:40.870 --> 00:55:43.150
for example, when
NSTAR was really

00:55:43.150 --> 00:55:44.850
misbehaving a few years ago.

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:50.920
So it's an issue everywhere
that things that seem reliable

00:55:50.920 --> 00:55:52.930
aren't always, and
then you start choosing

00:55:52.930 --> 00:55:55.750
different types of reliability.

00:55:55.750 --> 00:56:05.270
So now I want to go through
some examples of lighting

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:06.590
and lighting tasks.

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:09.800
So learning is
obviously a huge area

00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:12.560
where light provides a
huge benefit to people.

00:56:12.560 --> 00:56:15.235
If you can be studying and/or
going to class at night,

00:56:15.235 --> 00:56:16.610
that frees up a
tremendous number

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:20.760
of hours in the day
that you can work.

00:56:20.760 --> 00:56:22.280
And similarly working.

00:56:22.280 --> 00:56:25.520
So I mentioned people
plucking flowers,

00:56:25.520 --> 00:56:32.030
people farming silk from
silkworms, and people cooking.

00:56:32.030 --> 00:56:34.370
All of these things
are really benefited

00:56:34.370 --> 00:56:37.130
from being able
to work at night.

00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:39.170
These two examples
are from SELCO,

00:56:39.170 --> 00:56:43.370
which we'll talk about
later in a few slides.

00:56:43.370 --> 00:56:44.760
Markets and vendors.

00:56:44.760 --> 00:56:47.810
So if you can sell at
night as well as the day,

00:56:47.810 --> 00:56:50.570
that opens up a lot
of potential profit

00:56:50.570 --> 00:56:52.850
and potential customers for you.

00:56:52.850 --> 00:56:55.340
And then we talked a little
bit about medical care

00:56:55.340 --> 00:56:58.640
and the benefit all the
way from having babies

00:56:58.640 --> 00:57:01.730
to emergency surgery where
having light at night

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:03.990
is really crucial.

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:07.850
And then just walking
and being safe at night.

00:57:07.850 --> 00:57:10.880
Anywhere from driving
to wandering it's

00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:14.390
really handy to have
light, unsurprisingly.

00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:16.940
And then if you think about
how much time you spend

00:57:16.940 --> 00:57:19.610
at night socializing
and how much

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:22.340
harder that would be if
there was no light to do so

00:57:22.340 --> 00:57:25.820
and how much less
time you'd have

00:57:25.820 --> 00:57:28.820
for interactions with your
peers and with your friends

00:57:28.820 --> 00:57:31.010
and family at night.

00:57:31.010 --> 00:57:35.180
So lights like the
street light provides

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:37.970
an opportunity for people to
start all of a sudden meeting

00:57:37.970 --> 00:57:41.760
and socializing and/or
working together.

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:42.980
So some examples of light.

00:57:42.980 --> 00:57:44.810
So I mentioned
the d.light, and I

00:57:44.810 --> 00:57:48.000
think this light has been
upgraded a little bit.

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:51.200
And is now called
the D10 or S10.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:53.540
Maybe the S10.

00:57:53.540 --> 00:57:57.200
But basically it's made by
the same company, d.light,

00:57:57.200 --> 00:58:02.420
that makes this light, but
this light is about $60.

00:58:02.420 --> 00:58:05.480
Maybe $40 in some places.

00:58:05.480 --> 00:58:08.970
And this light they're
selling at around $10,

00:58:08.970 --> 00:58:11.960
and so it's a whole
lot more affordable.

00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:15.590
It doesn't offer the cell phone
charging and similar aspects,

00:58:15.590 --> 00:58:18.200
but they've also worked
a lot about making sure

00:58:18.200 --> 00:58:21.740
that it offers a really
good quality light.

00:58:21.740 --> 00:58:26.280
Some of the limitations of
this light, for example,

00:58:26.280 --> 00:58:28.670
were really clear in
just how glaring it was.

00:58:28.670 --> 00:58:31.190
And it's nice to
have light at all,

00:58:31.190 --> 00:58:33.780
but glaring light isn't ideal.

00:58:33.780 --> 00:58:36.380
And so they've done
a lot with diffusion

00:58:36.380 --> 00:58:38.820
to make sure that this
light is pleasant to use.

00:58:38.820 --> 00:58:41.300
And this is a nice
side by side picture

00:58:41.300 --> 00:58:45.050
of using a kerosene lantern
versus using a d.light.

00:58:45.050 --> 00:58:48.720
You can see the difference.

00:58:48.720 --> 00:58:51.530
This is much, much brighter
and probably a whole lot more

00:58:51.530 --> 00:58:55.550
pleasurable to read by as
long as it's not too glaring.

00:58:55.550 --> 00:58:58.850
And they also had
two settings so

00:58:58.850 --> 00:59:02.270
that you can save your
batteries if you need to.

00:59:02.270 --> 00:59:03.440
Grameen Shakti.

00:59:03.440 --> 00:59:06.290
You may have heard
of Grameen Bank,

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:09.020
and Grameen Shakti
is a related program

00:59:09.020 --> 00:59:13.280
where they've been providing
solar electrification primarily

00:59:13.280 --> 00:59:18.230
for lighting to
many, many homes.

00:59:18.230 --> 00:59:20.660
And so they're one
of classic example

00:59:20.660 --> 00:59:25.440
of focusing on PV systems
to provide lighting.

00:59:25.440 --> 00:59:28.610
SELCO, which I'm going to show
a video on about in a couple

00:59:28.610 --> 00:59:34.380
of minutes, is another
example where, as I mentioned,

00:59:34.380 --> 00:59:36.530
so they were using
kerosene lamps

00:59:36.530 --> 00:59:39.920
to feed silkworms, which
they needed to do at night.

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:43.730
And kerosene lamps, as you
can imagine, are dangerous.

00:59:43.730 --> 00:59:45.655
Not only are they smelly
and toxic to people,

00:59:45.655 --> 00:59:47.030
but they're killing
off silkworms

00:59:47.030 --> 00:59:48.740
because they're toxic
to silkworms too.

00:59:48.740 --> 00:59:53.930
And so SELCO is able to develop
a solar lantern system that

00:59:53.930 --> 00:59:57.620
became affordable through
some complicated financing

00:59:57.620 --> 00:59:58.820
methods, which is--

00:59:58.820 --> 01:00:01.580
SELCO does a combination
of technology and financing

01:00:01.580 --> 01:00:03.830
that allows people to access
solar lighting that they

01:00:03.830 --> 01:00:09.560
couldn't otherwise access and
it has had a lot of benefits.

01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:11.990
Similarly, solar
powered headlamps.

01:00:11.990 --> 01:00:15.440
The story on this project
is that initially midwives

01:00:15.440 --> 01:00:19.280
came to SELCO saying, we really
need solar powered lanterns--

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:20.840
like headlamps,
not just lanterns.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:23.630
Because if you can imagine,
when you're delivering a baby

01:00:23.630 --> 01:00:25.970
you need task lighting
front and center.

01:00:25.970 --> 01:00:28.160
And if you have a
lantern behind you

01:00:28.160 --> 01:00:30.350
your body is blocking
what you need to see,

01:00:30.350 --> 01:00:32.540
and so it becomes
very inefficient.

01:00:32.540 --> 01:00:35.030
And you don't want a kerosene
lamp right up and close.

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:37.010
That's just dangerous.

01:00:37.010 --> 01:00:40.130
And so SELCO came
up with a headlamp,

01:00:40.130 --> 01:00:42.980
but unfortunately, midwives
don't have enough clients

01:00:42.980 --> 01:00:44.180
to pay for the headlamp.

01:00:44.180 --> 01:00:47.150
So then SELCO looked at, well,
who else needs these headlamps?

01:00:47.150 --> 01:00:50.870
And they came up with a headlamp
sharing method so that people--

01:00:50.870 --> 01:00:54.620
flower pluckers, construction
workers, rubber tappers,

01:00:54.620 --> 01:00:56.960
and midwives could all
share the headlamps

01:00:56.960 --> 01:00:59.210
so that everyone could benefit.

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:02.280
So that requires not only some
special financing, but also

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:05.100
some special
community arrangements

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:06.600
so that everyone
is able to share

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:08.700
these headlamps successfully.

01:01:08.700 --> 01:01:12.055
But when they're successful,
it helps tremendously

01:01:12.055 --> 01:01:13.305
for a lot of different people.

01:01:17.200 --> 01:01:20.400
So now I want to
talk about SELCO

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:22.350
or show you a video about SELCO.

01:01:22.350 --> 01:01:24.287
The founder is Harish Hande.

01:01:24.287 --> 01:01:26.370
And I think the thing that
isn't going to come out

01:01:26.370 --> 01:01:31.135
in the video enough is what he
really learned very quickly,

01:01:31.135 --> 01:01:33.510
but had to do a lot of work
to figure out how to address,

01:01:33.510 --> 01:01:36.330
is that he had people
regularly telling him

01:01:36.330 --> 01:01:41.250
that 300 rupees a month is
expensive, but 10 rupees a day

01:01:41.250 --> 01:01:43.260
is fine.

01:01:43.260 --> 01:01:45.840
And you can do the math
really quickly and see

01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:47.640
that those are the same, right?

01:01:47.640 --> 01:01:51.030
That 30 times 10 is 300.

01:01:51.030 --> 01:01:54.420
So why do you think
one would say that?

01:01:58.545 --> 01:02:01.230
AUDIENCE: If you're
paying a sum--

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:05.730
firstly, just psychologically
it seems like more money

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:08.760
because 300 is
much more than 10.

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:12.330
But also because,
I mean, I think

01:02:12.330 --> 01:02:15.840
it's hard to-- it's easier
to budget day to day.

01:02:15.840 --> 01:02:18.610
It's hard to budget
an entire month.

01:02:18.610 --> 01:02:24.570
So it's probably easier to save
10 rupees every day as opposed

01:02:24.570 --> 01:02:27.270
to trying to save
300 for a month

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:29.340
because you're unsure
of a lot of variables.

01:02:29.340 --> 01:02:30.132
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:02:30.132 --> 01:02:32.670
So the budgeting cycle is
actually the huge thing.

01:02:32.670 --> 01:02:37.050
That people using kerosene
lanterns would sell during

01:02:37.050 --> 01:02:40.890
the day and if they made enough
to buy kerosene at night,

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:42.870
they would and if they
didn't, they wouldn't.

01:02:42.870 --> 01:02:47.850
And so that gave them a lot of
flexibility for their budget.

01:02:47.850 --> 01:02:53.340
Whereas if you spend 300 rupees
and then you have a bad month,

01:02:53.340 --> 01:02:55.860
you're out, and
there's no ability

01:02:55.860 --> 01:02:58.980
to optimize that
on a short cycle.

01:02:58.980 --> 01:03:01.320
AUDIENCE: I mean, I was
thinking that storing that money

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:03.283
somewhere [INAUDIBLE]
business because you

01:03:03.283 --> 01:03:05.200
don't want to have saved
up for a whole month.

01:03:05.200 --> 01:03:05.680
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:03:05.680 --> 01:03:06.180
So--

01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:10.420
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] budgeting
as picking general habits.

01:03:10.420 --> 01:03:12.640
And now you saved up the
whole month and somebody

01:03:12.640 --> 01:03:15.700
steals it or it's lost or burned
up because of the kerosene

01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:16.768
or whatever.

01:03:16.768 --> 01:03:17.560
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:03:17.560 --> 01:03:20.110
So saving is risky
and it's also hard.

01:03:20.110 --> 01:03:24.470
If you've never been
brought up to be budgeting--

01:03:24.470 --> 01:03:26.950
which is something that if
you live in poverty is not

01:03:26.950 --> 01:03:29.200
something that you're really
doing on a month to month

01:03:29.200 --> 01:03:32.320
or year to year basis, but
are living day to day--

01:03:32.320 --> 01:03:34.720
shifting to that mindset
is very difficult

01:03:34.720 --> 01:03:37.683
and it does add an element of
risk if you're saving money.

01:03:37.683 --> 01:03:39.850
You have to save it somewhere
that has to be secure,

01:03:39.850 --> 01:03:41.530
which is a big assumption.

01:03:41.530 --> 01:03:42.385
Any other thoughts?

01:03:46.080 --> 01:03:47.430
And this is Harish's quote.

01:03:47.430 --> 01:03:50.520
But having talked to
him, my understanding

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:56.370
is that 300 rupees a month is
expensive, but 20 rupees a day

01:03:56.370 --> 01:03:57.010
is fine.

01:03:57.010 --> 01:04:01.110
So people are really
generally spending much more

01:04:01.110 --> 01:04:04.377
to live day to day
than they are if they

01:04:04.377 --> 01:04:05.460
could live month to month.

01:04:05.460 --> 01:04:07.440
But living month to
month is a barrier

01:04:07.440 --> 01:04:12.480
so high that it's completely
insurmountable for people

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:16.360
at a certain level
of poverty and below.

01:04:16.360 --> 01:04:19.046
So I'm going to switch again.

01:04:19.046 --> 01:04:20.480
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:04:20.480 --> 01:04:23.990
- Street markets are a crucial
part of Indian commerce.

01:04:23.990 --> 01:04:26.870
Street vendors sell
everything from vegetables

01:04:26.870 --> 01:04:31.280
to incense to clothing
to handicrafts.

01:04:31.280 --> 01:04:34.790
Much of the action takes
place in the hours after dusk.

01:04:34.790 --> 01:04:37.700
Normally the vendors
depend on kerosene lamps

01:04:37.700 --> 01:04:39.720
to light their wares.

01:04:39.720 --> 01:04:42.470
SELCO thought that the street
vendors would be better served

01:04:42.470 --> 01:04:45.020
by solar lighting,
but the vendors

01:04:45.020 --> 01:04:48.560
were in no position to purchase
PV panels and batteries

01:04:48.560 --> 01:04:50.480
for solar lighting.

01:04:50.480 --> 01:04:53.090
So SELCO redesigned
the value chain

01:04:53.090 --> 01:04:56.930
and created a new class of
vendor, the solar lighting

01:04:56.930 --> 01:04:58.970
entrepreneur.

01:04:58.970 --> 01:05:01.400
With financing
arranged by SELCO,

01:05:01.400 --> 01:05:04.490
solar light entrepreneurs
purchased large arrays

01:05:04.490 --> 01:05:06.590
of solar panels
that can be placed

01:05:06.590 --> 01:05:09.740
on a roof or even a vacant lot.

01:05:09.740 --> 01:05:12.200
Every morning, the
solar entrepreneurs

01:05:12.200 --> 01:05:16.100
connect a series of
batteries to their PV panels.

01:05:16.100 --> 01:05:21.530
The 12 volt batteries are the
size and heft of car batteries.

01:05:21.530 --> 01:05:25.130
The entrepreneurs have a set
list of clients in the market.

01:05:25.130 --> 01:05:29.690
Some get just batteries, others
get both battery and lights.

01:05:29.690 --> 01:05:32.450
The entrepreneur we
visited, Dada Hayat,

01:05:32.450 --> 01:05:34.100
has over 50 clients.

01:05:37.550 --> 01:05:41.630
At 4:30 the solar entrepreneur
disconnects the batteries,

01:05:41.630 --> 01:05:45.620
loads his motorized rickshaw,
and drives his wares

01:05:45.620 --> 01:05:47.975
to the market a few
kilometers away.

01:05:53.740 --> 01:05:56.200
He then delivers the
solar powered batteries

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:56.950
to his clients.

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:08.500
For the vendors, the solar
lights prove to be a good deal.

01:06:08.500 --> 01:06:10.690
An the evening's
worth of light costs

01:06:10.690 --> 01:06:16.030
about 15% less than kerosene
would for the same time period.

01:06:16.030 --> 01:06:19.630
The lights have other advantages
over kerosene as well.

01:06:19.630 --> 01:06:23.140
They cast a wider arc
of light, don't smell,

01:06:23.140 --> 01:06:25.045
and do not pose a fire hazard.

01:06:27.880 --> 01:06:31.000
For solar entrepreneurs
such as Dada Hayat,

01:06:31.000 --> 01:06:33.880
the lights are also a good deal.

01:06:33.880 --> 01:06:37.600
After making his loan payment
and paying off other expenses,

01:06:37.600 --> 01:06:40.600
Hayat has enough
left over to live on.

01:06:40.600 --> 01:06:43.180
Moreover, he also
has the possibility

01:06:43.180 --> 01:06:45.430
of further expanding
his business.

01:06:45.430 --> 01:06:46.792
[END PLAYBACK]

01:06:46.792 --> 01:06:47.500
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

01:06:47.500 --> 01:06:52.630
So I think one of the things
that's amazing about SELCO

01:06:52.630 --> 01:06:58.540
is that the idea of someone
basically renting a light

01:06:58.540 --> 01:07:01.540
or access to light
every day kind of seems

01:07:01.540 --> 01:07:05.530
obvious and simple now in 2020
hindsight, but at the time

01:07:05.530 --> 01:07:06.950
is quite radical.

01:07:06.950 --> 01:07:09.770
And you can kind of imagine why.

01:07:09.770 --> 01:07:12.040
It's impossible
for me to imagine

01:07:12.040 --> 01:07:16.450
every day stopping by the
market and renting a light

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:20.800
to use tonight and tomorrow
bring it back and repeating.

01:07:20.800 --> 01:07:25.000
That's so contrary
to our societal norm

01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:26.840
that it's hard to
wrap your head around.

01:07:26.840 --> 01:07:29.200
And so this was a really
different approach

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:32.170
that turned out to be
very, very successful

01:07:32.170 --> 01:07:35.247
and is a great example
of an approach.

01:07:35.247 --> 01:07:37.330
And why are we talking
about this in energy class?

01:07:37.330 --> 01:07:39.820
Well, any solution has
the technology piece,

01:07:39.820 --> 01:07:41.410
but it also has
the business piece.

01:07:41.410 --> 01:07:43.660
And while we're certainly
not emphasizing the business

01:07:43.660 --> 01:07:45.952
piece-- and if you want to,
there's other D-Lab classes

01:07:45.952 --> 01:07:47.260
where you can focus on that--

01:07:47.260 --> 01:07:49.450
you can't design in
a vacuum assuming

01:07:49.450 --> 01:07:52.120
that if the technology is
good enough they will come,

01:07:52.120 --> 01:07:53.317
regardless of the financing.

01:07:53.317 --> 01:07:55.900
Just as you can't assume there's
a good enough financing model

01:07:55.900 --> 01:07:58.220
that's good enough if
the technology isn't.

01:07:58.220 --> 01:08:01.120
And if you watch a bunch
of Harish Hande's talking,

01:08:01.120 --> 01:08:05.540
he gets to that, that you need
both in order to be successful.

01:08:05.540 --> 01:08:08.380
So that wraps up the
lighting lecture that I have.

01:08:08.380 --> 01:08:10.900
Are there any
questions on lighting?

01:08:10.900 --> 01:08:14.710
So I know you guys are really
interested in the community

01:08:14.710 --> 01:08:17.319
partners we'll be working
with and what exactly we'll

01:08:17.319 --> 01:08:18.279
be doing.

01:08:18.279 --> 01:08:22.740
So I wanted to go through first
what students did last year.

01:08:22.740 --> 01:08:26.080
Amit, do you mind bringing
those three products over?

01:08:26.080 --> 01:08:30.520
So one team worked with
Blue Energy and Blue

01:08:30.520 --> 01:08:32.350
Field, which is on
the Atlantic coast,

01:08:32.350 --> 01:08:37.149
where they have a lot of
batteries, these deep cycle

01:08:37.149 --> 01:08:42.189
batteries, that need to
be recharged regularly.

01:08:42.189 --> 01:08:46.060
And the batteries,
they need to be

01:08:46.060 --> 01:08:47.620
recharged with distilled water.

01:08:47.620 --> 01:08:49.370
And if you don't
use distilled water

01:08:49.370 --> 01:08:51.040
you shorten the life
of the batteries

01:08:51.040 --> 01:08:53.229
by a really significant
amount, and these

01:08:53.229 --> 01:08:55.290
are very expensive
batteries that make up

01:08:55.290 --> 01:08:57.279
a big percentage of the system.

01:08:57.279 --> 01:09:01.420
And people are living in
islands and coastal areas

01:09:01.420 --> 01:09:04.720
where the only way to get
to buy distilled water

01:09:04.720 --> 01:09:08.569
is to travel by boat for a
few hours, which is expensive,

01:09:08.569 --> 01:09:11.600
and when there's any sort
of weather, is impossible.

01:09:11.600 --> 01:09:14.290
And so people will naturally
just use regular water

01:09:14.290 --> 01:09:15.790
rather than distilled water.

01:09:15.790 --> 01:09:18.790
And by regular water I generally
mean well water or worst case,

01:09:18.790 --> 01:09:21.880
ocean water, which kills the
batteries really quickly.

01:09:21.880 --> 01:09:23.892
But there's a
temptation to just--

01:09:23.892 --> 01:09:25.600
I know we need to fill
the battery so let

01:09:25.600 --> 01:09:26.830
me do it with the
water I have here

01:09:26.830 --> 01:09:28.310
if I can't get distilled water.

01:09:28.310 --> 01:09:31.130
Distilled water also isn't
free if you're buying it.

01:09:31.130 --> 01:09:33.580
And so what they developed
were two different approaches

01:09:33.580 --> 01:09:38.540
to a distiller at two different
cost prices or price levels.

01:09:38.540 --> 01:09:41.500
So one using acrylic
panels and one just

01:09:41.500 --> 01:09:43.810
using clear plastic sheeting.

01:09:43.810 --> 01:09:49.120
And then you just put it over
a water source and the water

01:09:49.120 --> 01:09:52.090
evaporates in the sun.

01:09:52.090 --> 01:09:55.570
And when it evaporates,
all the stuff

01:09:55.570 --> 01:10:00.760
that's not just pure water
is left in the pan down here

01:10:00.760 --> 01:10:04.030
and everything that is
rolling down the sides

01:10:04.030 --> 01:10:07.090
is distilled water
and then it collects.

01:10:07.090 --> 01:10:10.150
This isn't a good option
for drinking water, both

01:10:10.150 --> 01:10:13.660
because distilled water tastes
awful and isn't as good for you

01:10:13.660 --> 01:10:15.430
as water that has
some minerals in it,

01:10:15.430 --> 01:10:17.350
and also because it's
a pretty slow process.

01:10:17.350 --> 01:10:18.808
But if you only
need a small amount

01:10:18.808 --> 01:10:22.300
of water per month for a
battery, this works really well

01:10:22.300 --> 01:10:26.020
and can be made pretty much out
of locally available materials

01:10:26.020 --> 01:10:27.940
and is pretty simple.

01:10:27.940 --> 01:10:30.280
And so that was one project
that the students worked on.

01:10:30.280 --> 01:10:33.140
What did they do when
they went to Blue Energy?

01:10:33.140 --> 01:10:36.248
We did a charcoal
demonstration for people

01:10:36.248 --> 01:10:38.290
so that they could learn
how to make charcoal out

01:10:38.290 --> 01:10:40.840
of coconut husks because that
was what was locally available.

01:10:40.840 --> 01:10:45.340
That involved going
to a coastal area that

01:10:45.340 --> 01:10:49.330
is one of those areas where you
can't get to except for boat

01:10:49.330 --> 01:10:51.040
called Kakabila.

01:10:51.040 --> 01:10:54.310
So we spent some time
there just learning

01:10:54.310 --> 01:10:58.810
what the energy situation was,
helping repair a wind turbine,

01:10:58.810 --> 01:11:01.305
and identifying other
projects in Blue Fields

01:11:01.305 --> 01:11:03.430
that we could work on, and
this is the project that

01:11:03.430 --> 01:11:04.600
ended up getting worked on.

01:11:04.600 --> 01:11:07.300
All of these projects are going
to be relatively small compared

01:11:07.300 --> 01:11:09.490
to, say, a 2009 project.

01:11:09.490 --> 01:11:10.880
The reason for that is twofold.

01:11:10.880 --> 01:11:12.670
One is that this class
we pretty much work

01:11:12.670 --> 01:11:14.170
on projects for
half the semester as

01:11:14.170 --> 01:11:16.000
opposed to the whole semester.

01:11:16.000 --> 01:11:17.890
And the other reason
is that I really

01:11:17.890 --> 01:11:19.940
want these projects
to get to completion,

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:21.533
so a project that
we can actually

01:11:21.533 --> 01:11:23.950
hand off to a community partner
at the end of the semester

01:11:23.950 --> 01:11:25.990
and say, here, run
with it, and they

01:11:25.990 --> 01:11:28.910
can without it falling
apart and just being like,

01:11:28.910 --> 01:11:30.010
oh, it doesn't work.

01:11:30.010 --> 01:11:33.490
And a lot of projects,
just naturally in academia,

01:11:33.490 --> 01:11:36.207
get to the, it just works well
enough for the presentation

01:11:36.207 --> 01:11:36.790
and that's it.

01:11:36.790 --> 01:11:39.635
And I want to project that will
work well enough to hand off,

01:11:39.635 --> 01:11:40.760
which is a different level.

01:11:40.760 --> 01:11:43.150
And so that's why we do
relatively smaller projects,

01:11:43.150 --> 01:11:47.580
but we get to into deeper
level of design on them.

01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:50.960
So the next one is a bag sealer.

01:11:50.960 --> 01:11:52.640
So this is working--

01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:54.650
oh, and Blue Energy
is likely a group

01:11:54.650 --> 01:11:56.990
we won't be able to
work with this year.

01:11:56.990 --> 01:11:58.580
But this group
worked with AsoFenix,

01:11:58.580 --> 01:12:02.360
which is a group we likely will
be able to work with this year.

01:12:02.360 --> 01:12:05.630
And AsoFenix, one
of their projects

01:12:05.630 --> 01:12:08.810
was to make a bag
sealer for orange juice.

01:12:08.810 --> 01:12:15.440
So in one of the regions
that AsoFenix works with,

01:12:15.440 --> 01:12:18.590
they have a microhydro
system, and they actually

01:12:18.590 --> 01:12:21.170
are under utilizing
that electricity

01:12:21.170 --> 01:12:22.815
that they're provided.

01:12:22.815 --> 01:12:24.440
The electricity
doesn't work year round

01:12:24.440 --> 01:12:26.148
because the water
isn't there year round,

01:12:26.148 --> 01:12:30.680
but in the rainy season they
have a plethora of electricity.

01:12:30.680 --> 01:12:33.470
And they also have a
plethora of oranges,

01:12:33.470 --> 01:12:35.990
and the orange growing
season is relatively short.

01:12:35.990 --> 01:12:40.753
So you have all these
oranges and you can sell them

01:12:40.753 --> 01:12:42.170
for very little
in the marketplace

01:12:42.170 --> 01:12:46.050
because all the oranges come
to season at the same time.

01:12:46.050 --> 01:12:48.230
And so they're
trying to figure out,

01:12:48.230 --> 01:12:50.490
how do we save these
oranges to save some money?

01:12:50.490 --> 01:12:53.660
And so they looked into
preserving the juice

01:12:53.660 --> 01:12:55.880
and then selling
the juice and being

01:12:55.880 --> 01:12:57.320
able to sell that
for longer than

01:12:57.320 --> 01:12:58.903
you would be able
to sell the oranges.

01:12:58.903 --> 01:13:01.190
And so the key piece
that was missing

01:13:01.190 --> 01:13:02.660
was, how do we seal the bags?

01:13:02.660 --> 01:13:06.050
And so bag sealing
technology exists,

01:13:06.050 --> 01:13:07.910
but having a simple
one that connects

01:13:07.910 --> 01:13:10.970
to a microhydroelectric
system that's easy for them

01:13:10.970 --> 01:13:13.330
to repair and build in
the field was something

01:13:13.330 --> 01:13:15.830
that didn't exist, and so that's
what this group of students

01:13:15.830 --> 01:13:16.490
worked on.

01:13:16.490 --> 01:13:19.010
And when they were
at AsoFenix, again,

01:13:19.010 --> 01:13:21.950
they did charcoal
demonstrations as well as

01:13:21.950 --> 01:13:23.500
identifying the
different projects

01:13:23.500 --> 01:13:24.500
that they could work on.

01:13:24.500 --> 01:13:27.252
In this year's class we're
doing it slightly differently,

01:13:27.252 --> 01:13:29.210
that we're going to
pre-talk about the projects

01:13:29.210 --> 01:13:32.600
and you're going to work on
them a little bit before you go.

01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:35.240
That year they were really just
doing project identification.

01:13:41.720 --> 01:13:43.550
This prototype has
already been delivered

01:13:43.550 --> 01:13:45.967
to Grupo Phoenix, which is
another one of the groups we're

01:13:45.967 --> 01:13:49.040
going to be working with,
so I can't show it apart

01:13:49.040 --> 01:13:50.630
from the picture.

01:13:50.630 --> 01:13:56.360
But this group
makes solar panels

01:13:56.360 --> 01:14:02.210
from cells that are reject
cells that don't meet

01:14:02.210 --> 01:14:03.830
the specs for Evergreen Solar.

01:14:03.830 --> 01:14:07.130
So Evergreen donates the
cells to a Grupo Phoenix

01:14:07.130 --> 01:14:10.070
and they cut them up and
make solar panels with them.

01:14:10.070 --> 01:14:14.250
And cutting a solar cell is
really, really difficult.

01:14:14.250 --> 01:14:17.300
And in the field or in the lab
you would do it with a laser,

01:14:17.300 --> 01:14:19.670
but a laser is well
beyond the capability

01:14:19.670 --> 01:14:23.180
of Grupo Phoenix, which is
off the grid, pretty much.

01:14:23.180 --> 01:14:28.070
And so this group developed
a fixture for a dremel

01:14:28.070 --> 01:14:31.640
to allow a more
repeatable process

01:14:31.640 --> 01:14:34.850
for cutting the cells so that
the loss rate went from, like,

01:14:34.850 --> 01:14:36.450
80% to 50%.

01:14:36.450 --> 01:14:38.450
So a big improvement.

01:14:38.450 --> 01:14:40.280
Even though 50% is
a painful loss rate,

01:14:40.280 --> 01:14:43.770
80% is much more painful.

01:14:43.770 --> 01:14:47.030
And then the last group worked
with one organization called

01:14:47.030 --> 01:14:49.370
Carré, which, again, we
probably won't work with again--

01:14:49.370 --> 01:14:51.710
and also for Grupo
Phoenix, which

01:14:51.710 --> 01:14:52.940
we are working with again--

01:14:52.940 --> 01:14:56.060
for a stove in a stove design.

01:14:56.060 --> 01:15:01.370
We introduced the charcoal idea
to Grupo Phoenix, women there.

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:03.230
Grupo Phoenix is
a neat community

01:15:03.230 --> 01:15:07.700
that has a very strong
organization of women,

01:15:07.700 --> 01:15:12.273
almost entirely,
and those women are

01:15:12.273 --> 01:15:13.940
really interested in
alternative energy.

01:15:13.940 --> 01:15:16.280
And so they build their
own solar cookers.

01:15:16.280 --> 01:15:18.150
The solar cookers
are really expensive

01:15:18.150 --> 01:15:19.580
and so that's a
problematic model

01:15:19.580 --> 01:15:21.320
from a financial
long-term standpoint.

01:15:21.320 --> 01:15:23.330
But from a community
building social standpoint,

01:15:23.330 --> 01:15:25.950
it's a really interesting,
very successful model.

01:15:25.950 --> 01:15:28.167
And so we're interested
in working on them

01:15:28.167 --> 01:15:30.500
to figure out what are some
other opportunities they can

01:15:30.500 --> 01:15:33.293
look into that might be
more sustainable long term,

01:15:33.293 --> 01:15:34.460
and one of them is charcoal.

01:15:34.460 --> 01:15:36.210
And they're really
interested in charcoal,

01:15:36.210 --> 01:15:38.300
but they all use
adobe stoves meant

01:15:38.300 --> 01:15:40.670
for wood, which aren't
appropriate for charcoal.

01:15:40.670 --> 01:15:43.820
And so I and the students
working on this project

01:15:43.820 --> 01:15:46.340
came up with the idea to
develop a stove in a stove.

01:15:46.340 --> 01:15:49.610
So a charcoal stove that would
pop into your adobe stove,

01:15:49.610 --> 01:15:52.160
wouldn't interrupt your
cooking, but would allow you

01:15:52.160 --> 01:15:53.810
to use charcoal very easily.

01:15:53.810 --> 01:15:55.550
And the students
came up with the idea

01:15:55.550 --> 01:15:58.580
of making sure the stove is
something that could be built

01:15:58.580 --> 01:16:01.160
by the women at Grupo Phoenix.

01:16:01.160 --> 01:16:03.200
So what they came
up with was a stove

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:05.390
that can be made from a
single piece of sheet metal

01:16:05.390 --> 01:16:08.270
with just a couple of cuts
and then a bunch of bending.

01:16:08.270 --> 01:16:13.150
And so they figured out the
stove idea over spring break,

01:16:13.150 --> 01:16:14.900
and then in the summer
one of the students

01:16:14.900 --> 01:16:18.440
went back and introduced the
stove, taught a workshop on how

01:16:18.440 --> 01:16:22.730
to make that, and tested
it with some women

01:16:22.730 --> 01:16:25.860
to see how it worked, which
is a pretty cool project.

01:16:25.860 --> 01:16:29.790
And so that's the
stove right here.

01:16:29.790 --> 01:16:36.170
So those are the four projects
that came out last year.

01:16:36.170 --> 01:16:40.520
And so this year we
fly into Managua,

01:16:40.520 --> 01:16:44.330
which is the capital city, but
we spend almost no time there.

01:16:44.330 --> 01:16:47.690
The Grupo Phoenix is in Okatal.

01:16:47.690 --> 01:16:49.910
We may work in Jinotega.

01:16:49.910 --> 01:16:53.855
And AsoFenix is based
generally in the Boaco region.

01:16:57.430 --> 01:17:01.270
So AsoFenix is a
small Nicaraguan

01:17:01.270 --> 01:17:04.990
based NGO focused on rural
sustainable development,

01:17:04.990 --> 01:17:06.760
and they do a lot
in the energy realm.

01:17:06.760 --> 01:17:09.680
They're actually a group that
was spun out of Grupo Phoenix,

01:17:09.680 --> 01:17:13.600
so that's why the
names sound so similar.

01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:16.150
It's in a very, very
rural area, which

01:17:16.150 --> 01:17:17.380
means you have to hike in.

01:17:17.380 --> 01:17:22.550
There's no way to even
get a good pickup in.

01:17:22.550 --> 01:17:25.750
And so it's pretty rustic
conditions, pit latrines,

01:17:25.750 --> 01:17:28.660
bucket showers if
there's enough water,

01:17:28.660 --> 01:17:31.630
and sleeping on hammocks.

01:17:31.630 --> 01:17:34.930
And one of their big projects
is they have a feed chopper

01:17:34.930 --> 01:17:37.480
and they want to be
able to electrify it

01:17:37.480 --> 01:17:39.130
because right now
it's human powered

01:17:39.130 --> 01:17:42.490
and it's a really, really
manually intensive system

01:17:42.490 --> 01:17:44.800
to use it.

01:17:44.800 --> 01:17:47.890
And then they also have
interest in biodigesters, maybe

01:17:47.890 --> 01:17:50.740
continuing work on the
bag sealing project.

01:17:50.740 --> 01:17:53.900
And they're currently
having a meeting this week,

01:17:53.900 --> 01:17:55.900
I believe, to talk about
what other projects

01:17:55.900 --> 01:17:57.550
they'd be interested in.

01:17:57.550 --> 01:17:59.020
That's one.

01:17:59.020 --> 01:18:02.950
Second is Grupo Phoenix,
which is in Okatal.

01:18:02.950 --> 01:18:07.840
And this is a group that has
a university base in Nicaragua

01:18:07.840 --> 01:18:10.240
at the Technical
University in Nicaragua,

01:18:10.240 --> 01:18:13.390
and has some connections
back to the D-Lab as well

01:18:13.390 --> 01:18:15.290
as other universities.

01:18:15.290 --> 01:18:19.900
So it's a little bit more
westernized just a touch,

01:18:19.900 --> 01:18:26.590
but it is very integrated into
the community with that women's

01:18:26.590 --> 01:18:28.790
cooperative that I mentioned.

01:18:28.790 --> 01:18:31.090
And so they're really
focused on trying

01:18:31.090 --> 01:18:34.930
to empower the women
to create better energy

01:18:34.930 --> 01:18:37.000
solutions for
themselves and preserve

01:18:37.000 --> 01:18:39.290
their natural resources.

01:18:39.290 --> 01:18:42.850
So this group has
comparative wealth

01:18:42.850 --> 01:18:45.435
in that some of the houses
have TVs, things like that.

01:18:45.435 --> 01:18:46.810
So some of our
students last year

01:18:46.810 --> 01:18:48.838
were surprised by how
wealthy with the people

01:18:48.838 --> 01:18:49.630
we're working with.

01:18:49.630 --> 01:18:52.172
One of the reasons that these
women are comparatively wealthy

01:18:52.172 --> 01:18:54.580
is that this program has
been going on for 20 years

01:18:54.580 --> 01:18:56.200
and some of the work
has allowed them

01:18:56.200 --> 01:18:58.090
to gain a little bit of wealth.

01:18:58.090 --> 01:19:02.620
But the neat thing about them is
that they serve as figureheads

01:19:02.620 --> 01:19:03.860
for the whole region.

01:19:03.860 --> 01:19:07.900
And so success that they
have gets other people really

01:19:07.900 --> 01:19:11.050
interested, and so it's a really
neat program to work with.

01:19:11.050 --> 01:19:14.590
Some of the projects they are
interested in-- but again,

01:19:14.590 --> 01:19:17.380
pit latrines, bucket
showers is the standard.

01:19:17.380 --> 01:19:19.750
But you do get a bed as
opposed to a hammock,

01:19:19.750 --> 01:19:22.870
so it's a little
bit more refined.

01:19:22.870 --> 01:19:25.900
Is refining their solar
cooker, ideally refining

01:19:25.900 --> 01:19:28.390
it significantly to
make it much more

01:19:28.390 --> 01:19:32.050
affordable, easy to manufacture,
aesthetically pleasing, et

01:19:32.050 --> 01:19:34.083
cetera, et cetera,
more functional.

01:19:34.083 --> 01:19:36.250
There's actually a group
of Cornell students working

01:19:36.250 --> 01:19:37.750
on aspects of that
as well, so it

01:19:37.750 --> 01:19:39.610
would be interesting-- it'll
be interesting to figure out

01:19:39.610 --> 01:19:40.480
how we intersect.

01:19:40.480 --> 01:19:43.400
But they're going to be at
Grupo Phoenix at the same time,

01:19:43.400 --> 01:19:46.660
so there would be an
easy way to overlap.

01:19:46.660 --> 01:19:48.910
They're also really
interested in charcoal making

01:19:48.910 --> 01:19:50.000
and the stove making.

01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:52.720
And so working with
them on those aspects,

01:19:52.720 --> 01:19:54.220
both from developing
their skills

01:19:54.220 --> 01:19:57.117
in making charcoal as well
as business plan development.

01:19:57.117 --> 01:19:59.200
And then they're really
interested in figuring out

01:19:59.200 --> 01:20:01.840
a good way to make cell
phone chargers because that's

01:20:01.840 --> 01:20:02.723
a huge--

01:20:02.723 --> 01:20:04.390
solar cell phone
chargers because that's

01:20:04.390 --> 01:20:08.248
a huge need in the area where
almost everyone has cell phones

01:20:08.248 --> 01:20:09.790
and very few people
have electricity,

01:20:09.790 --> 01:20:13.508
so charging their cell
phones is really difficult.

01:20:13.508 --> 01:20:15.550
Other possibilities that
we're looking into right

01:20:15.550 --> 01:20:19.180
now are collaborating
with D-Lab Health

01:20:19.180 --> 01:20:21.880
because D-Lab Health has
a lot of other projects

01:20:21.880 --> 01:20:24.307
and they also go to Nicaragua
and so it seems natural

01:20:24.307 --> 01:20:25.390
that we could collaborate.

01:20:25.390 --> 01:20:28.240
And they often have energy
needs for their solutions,

01:20:28.240 --> 01:20:29.990
and so it seems like
a natural overlap.

01:20:29.990 --> 01:20:32.860
So I'm talking to them, but
José, who teaches the class,

01:20:32.860 --> 01:20:36.182
just got back from Nicaragua
yesterday so I haven't been

01:20:36.182 --> 01:20:37.640
able to talk to
him about that yet.

01:20:37.640 --> 01:20:42.200
And then I visited
Jinotega this summer,

01:20:42.200 --> 01:20:44.230
which is another
region, and they're

01:20:44.230 --> 01:20:45.730
really interested
in making charcoal

01:20:45.730 --> 01:20:50.800
from these invasive
lilies in their lake.

01:20:50.800 --> 01:20:52.550
And so that's
another possibility.

01:20:52.550 --> 01:20:54.267
But I don't have
strong enough contacts

01:20:54.267 --> 01:20:56.350
to be able to be sure that
that's going to happen,

01:20:56.350 --> 01:20:58.698
but it is a possibility
that I'm working on.

01:20:58.698 --> 01:21:00.490
Are there any questions
about all of these?

01:21:05.680 --> 01:21:06.490
OK.

01:21:06.490 --> 01:21:10.090
So by Friday, I will
have a survey online

01:21:10.090 --> 01:21:13.060
so that you can list
your project preferences.

01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:14.830
And I know these are
relatively vague,

01:21:14.830 --> 01:21:18.100
but that's the
nature of this work.

01:21:18.100 --> 01:21:20.320
And then I'll need you to
complete that by Monday.

01:21:20.320 --> 01:21:21.820
Even if you're not
going on the trip

01:21:21.820 --> 01:21:23.583
I need your preference
because you're

01:21:23.583 --> 01:21:25.000
going to be working
on the project

01:21:25.000 --> 01:21:26.950
apart from going on the trip.

01:21:26.950 --> 01:21:29.710
And then we'll have the
teams formed by the 23rd.

01:21:29.710 --> 01:21:32.800
And then from there you'll
be assigned a project mentor

01:21:32.800 --> 01:21:34.570
and you guys will be
working in your teams

01:21:34.570 --> 01:21:40.300
to work on developing initial
information, information

01:21:40.300 --> 01:21:42.580
gathering, initial
prototypes, that you can bring

01:21:42.580 --> 01:21:46.000
to Nicaragua to help you have
more informed conversations

01:21:46.000 --> 01:21:49.000
with your community partners
and make a better end

01:21:49.000 --> 01:21:50.830
product in the end.

01:21:50.830 --> 01:21:55.180
The other aspect of work that
may be happening in Nicaragua

01:21:55.180 --> 01:21:57.675
in addition to gathering
information about your project,

01:21:57.675 --> 01:21:59.800
so get getting feedback on
your initial prototypes,

01:21:59.800 --> 01:22:02.470
your initial
approaches, is trying

01:22:02.470 --> 01:22:05.260
to transfer some
of our technologies

01:22:05.260 --> 01:22:07.280
to the community partners
we're working with.

01:22:07.280 --> 01:22:10.308
So for example, Grupo Phoenix
is really interested in doing

01:22:10.308 --> 01:22:11.350
some more charcoal burns.

01:22:11.350 --> 01:22:12.725
So we'll do that
at Grupo Phoenix

01:22:12.725 --> 01:22:14.740
and you'll be trained
on charcoal burns right

01:22:14.740 --> 01:22:16.990
before we go, because we
try to push that as late as

01:22:16.990 --> 01:22:21.220
possible so that we're
not doing it in the snow.

01:22:21.220 --> 01:22:23.135
I haven't had a chance
to talk to AsoFenix

01:22:23.135 --> 01:22:24.760
if they want to do
charcoal burns again

01:22:24.760 --> 01:22:26.677
or if there's some other
technology they want.

01:22:26.677 --> 01:22:30.830
The primary work will be
fleshing out the projects

01:22:30.830 --> 01:22:32.830
and getting project
feedback, but there may also

01:22:32.830 --> 01:22:36.820
be some tech transfer,
but not a ton.

01:22:36.820 --> 01:22:39.940
So does that answer everyone's
questions about all of this?

01:22:39.940 --> 01:22:41.740
Because there were a
lot on the muddy cards

01:22:41.740 --> 01:22:44.185
so I just want to make
sure that's clearer.

01:22:48.270 --> 01:22:49.020
OK.

01:22:49.020 --> 01:22:49.650
Cool.

01:22:49.650 --> 01:22:55.920
So now I want to go through
the muddy cards again,

01:22:55.920 --> 01:22:57.600
which I try to do every week.

01:22:57.600 --> 01:23:00.480
One person asked, what is
the average daily energy

01:23:00.480 --> 01:23:01.800
consumption for Americans?

01:23:01.800 --> 01:23:06.270
Which hopefully you're
intuiting at this point.

01:23:06.270 --> 01:23:09.270
I showed it on the
first week slides.

01:23:09.270 --> 01:23:11.140
It's relatively easy to look up.

01:23:11.140 --> 01:23:15.510
But the average American uses
about 14,000 kilowatt hours

01:23:15.510 --> 01:23:17.520
per year of electricity.

01:23:17.520 --> 01:23:20.190
So that's not all energy
that we consume, obviously,

01:23:20.190 --> 01:23:21.990
but it gives you a baseline.

01:23:25.290 --> 01:23:30.120
Someone was confused about how
megajoules and kilowatt hours

01:23:30.120 --> 01:23:31.240
are related.

01:23:31.240 --> 01:23:37.650
So they're both units of
energy and it's 3.6 megajoules

01:23:37.650 --> 01:23:40.050
is 1 kilowatt hour.

01:23:44.100 --> 01:23:47.190
And generally power and
energy intuition is muddy,

01:23:47.190 --> 01:23:54.682
so can you guys remind
me, energy is equivalent--

01:23:54.682 --> 01:23:56.640
can you do a quick
definition of the difference

01:23:56.640 --> 01:23:59.585
between energy or
power or a metaphor

01:23:59.585 --> 01:24:00.960
or however you
want to define it,

01:24:00.960 --> 01:24:02.835
but how are you guys
keeping it in your mind?

01:24:08.020 --> 01:24:11.840
Or it's muddy for everyone?

01:24:11.840 --> 01:24:13.298
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:13.298 --> 01:24:14.090
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:24:14.090 --> 01:24:16.250
Yeah.

01:24:16.250 --> 01:24:17.930
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:17.930 --> 01:24:19.370
AMY BANZAERT: Excuse me?

01:24:19.370 --> 01:24:22.280
AUDIENCE: Power is like velocity
and energy is like distance.

01:24:22.280 --> 01:24:24.620
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:24:24.620 --> 01:24:31.860
And energy is
proportional to distance.

01:24:31.860 --> 01:24:36.290
So power has-- you're
considering time and energy,

01:24:36.290 --> 01:24:39.170
you're just considering the net.

01:24:39.170 --> 01:24:43.890
And yeah, if this is
feeling uncomfortable,

01:24:43.890 --> 01:24:46.910
I would practice it
for the first quiz

01:24:46.910 --> 01:24:48.890
so you get that into your head.

01:24:48.890 --> 01:24:51.590
And come talk to Amit
or myself if you want

01:24:51.590 --> 01:24:53.327
to spend some more time on it.

01:24:53.327 --> 01:24:54.660
AUDIENCE: The quiz is next week?

01:24:54.660 --> 01:24:56.030
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so I'll
talk about that in a minute.

01:24:56.030 --> 01:24:56.572
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:24:59.150 --> 01:25:02.810
AMY BANZAERT: So confused
about the dissipation of energy

01:25:02.810 --> 01:25:08.580
in the grid and having
the wrong length of wire.

01:25:08.580 --> 01:25:11.930
So basically what we
talked about last week was

01:25:11.930 --> 01:25:16.430
that the longer the
wire, the more--

01:25:16.430 --> 01:25:18.290
the bigger resistor you have.

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:20.900
And the bigger resistor you
have, the more heat loss

01:25:20.900 --> 01:25:21.620
you have.

01:25:21.620 --> 01:25:24.740
And the more heat loss you
have, the less energy transfer

01:25:24.740 --> 01:25:28.460
you have because more of it is
going into heat and less of it

01:25:28.460 --> 01:25:31.430
is getting to your end
source and your end use.

01:25:31.430 --> 01:25:34.460
So that was the basic
gist of last time

01:25:34.460 --> 01:25:37.880
was that you're never going to
be able to transfer electricity

01:25:37.880 --> 01:25:39.992
perfectly from one
system to another

01:25:39.992 --> 01:25:42.200
because there's always going
to be efficiency losses.

01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:44.720
But the farther distance
you have to travel,

01:25:44.720 --> 01:25:46.350
the more loss you're
going to have.

01:25:46.350 --> 01:25:48.710
And so you need to either
minimize the distance

01:25:48.710 --> 01:25:52.280
or minimize the resistance
associated with a wire in order

01:25:52.280 --> 01:25:55.790
to have a successful transfer.

01:25:55.790 --> 01:25:57.740
I hope that clarifies
it a little bit.

01:25:57.740 --> 01:26:00.080
If not, again, come talk to us.

01:26:03.410 --> 01:26:06.930
Still a little confused
about batteries.

01:26:06.930 --> 01:26:10.700
Again, talk to us because that's
too general a question for me

01:26:10.700 --> 01:26:14.840
to know what to answer,
but hopefully we

01:26:14.840 --> 01:26:17.120
can sort it out together.

01:26:17.120 --> 01:26:20.390
And then estimations of my
daily energy consumption

01:26:20.390 --> 01:26:23.090
would be easier with
given values of wattage

01:26:23.090 --> 01:26:24.410
for common appliances.

01:26:24.410 --> 01:26:25.220
Yup.

01:26:25.220 --> 01:26:26.000
Sorry.

01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:29.360
So the whole idea for this
was to practice the estimation

01:26:29.360 --> 01:26:31.430
and then use those
estimates to do

01:26:31.430 --> 01:26:34.107
your first pass of your
daily energy consumption,

01:26:34.107 --> 01:26:35.690
but then have the
time on the homework

01:26:35.690 --> 01:26:39.560
to refine those estimates
with actual real numbers.

01:26:39.560 --> 01:26:42.210
But the intent is to
practice those estimations,

01:26:42.210 --> 01:26:44.990
so that's why I didn't
just give them to you.

01:26:44.990 --> 01:26:48.170
And then one last question
was the source differences

01:26:48.170 --> 01:26:49.820
between--

01:26:49.820 --> 01:26:51.740
we talked about the
load differences,

01:26:51.740 --> 01:26:53.360
but we didn't talk
about the sources.

01:26:53.360 --> 01:26:55.610
And so we were doing
a black box source,

01:26:55.610 --> 01:26:59.150
and so we'll talk about solar
power in more detail and wind

01:26:59.150 --> 01:27:01.820
power in more detail coming up.

01:27:01.820 --> 01:27:05.060
But yeah, right now
we're thinking about it

01:27:05.060 --> 01:27:08.600
as a black box, so that's
why it's muddy because I

01:27:08.600 --> 01:27:11.840
haven't made it less muddy yet.

01:27:11.840 --> 01:27:12.440
OK.

01:27:12.440 --> 01:27:14.190
Are there any questions
about all of that?

01:27:17.580 --> 01:27:19.850
So one thing I wanted
to mention just

01:27:19.850 --> 01:27:22.640
to finish up on the Q
Drum, because I didn't want

01:27:22.640 --> 01:27:25.400
to talk about it too much
until you guys did all

01:27:25.400 --> 01:27:30.170
your readings and stuff, is
some of the criticisms of the Q

01:27:30.170 --> 01:27:31.370
Drum.

01:27:31.370 --> 01:27:32.930
And I also show the
Hippo Roller just

01:27:32.930 --> 01:27:36.930
so you see a different version
but a similar approach.

01:27:36.930 --> 01:27:39.590
So what do you think some of
the criticisms of the Q Drum

01:27:39.590 --> 01:27:41.028
might be?

01:27:41.028 --> 01:27:41.872
AUDIENCE: Leaks.

01:27:41.872 --> 01:27:42.830
AMY BANZAERT: It leaks.

01:27:42.830 --> 01:27:43.330
Yeah.

01:27:43.330 --> 01:27:50.240
So once it starts leaking,
it's a pretty crummy technology

01:27:50.240 --> 01:27:51.270
to have.

01:27:51.270 --> 01:27:51.770
Yeah.

01:27:51.770 --> 01:27:53.558
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:27:53.558 --> 01:27:54.350
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:27:54.350 --> 01:27:57.740
So on terrain like
is shown by that boy

01:27:57.740 --> 01:28:00.230
it's pretty
manageable, but stairs

01:28:00.230 --> 01:28:02.060
were sort of an extreme
version of terrain.

01:28:02.060 --> 01:28:04.670
But you can imagine
in super steep areas

01:28:04.670 --> 01:28:06.185
it's not so fun to use.

01:28:08.940 --> 01:28:10.655
So terrain is one concern.

01:28:13.700 --> 01:28:16.130
The longevity, the leaking.

01:28:16.130 --> 01:28:17.433
What else?

01:28:17.433 --> 01:28:19.225
AUDIENCE: I mean, the
water could get dirty

01:28:19.225 --> 01:28:22.225
because of where the valve is--

01:28:22.225 --> 01:28:23.268
or where the cap is.

01:28:23.268 --> 01:28:24.060
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:28:24.060 --> 01:28:30.440
So that's a really
big issue on a Q Drum

01:28:30.440 --> 01:28:35.580
because you have your valve
right at the edge of the drum.

01:28:35.580 --> 01:28:39.030
And so your valve is rolling
in the mud, basically.

01:28:39.030 --> 01:28:40.940
And so every time
you open and close

01:28:40.940 --> 01:28:44.910
the drum you're getting
dirt into the drum,

01:28:44.910 --> 01:28:46.430
which makes it dirtier.

01:28:46.430 --> 01:28:47.930
Someone in this
classroom told me

01:28:47.930 --> 01:28:51.530
that the Hippo Roller now
has a UV light inside it

01:28:51.530 --> 01:28:53.060
to try to address that issue.

01:28:53.060 --> 01:28:56.540
They also have their valve a
whole lot higher up in terms

01:28:56.540 --> 01:28:59.130
of water cleanliness issues.

01:28:59.130 --> 01:29:01.310
So that is one of the
limitations of the Q Drum

01:29:01.310 --> 01:29:05.000
is just how dirty your water
has the potential to get.

01:29:05.000 --> 01:29:07.820
And it's pretty near
impossible to clean out

01:29:07.820 --> 01:29:13.400
a Q Drum successfully because
of the way it's constructed.

01:29:13.400 --> 01:29:15.310
Any other thoughts?

01:29:15.310 --> 01:29:15.810
Yeah?

01:29:15.810 --> 01:29:17.490
AUDIENCE: It takes
physically more materials

01:29:17.490 --> 01:29:19.350
to construct, say, a
five gallon buckets,

01:29:19.350 --> 01:29:20.808
and it's not as versatile.

01:29:20.808 --> 01:29:21.600
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:29:21.600 --> 01:29:26.080
So a whole lot less versatile
and expensive and prevalent,

01:29:26.080 --> 01:29:26.580
right?

01:29:26.580 --> 01:29:28.320
Like you can find a
five gallon bucket

01:29:28.320 --> 01:29:30.940
everywhere, pretty
much, or the equivalent.

01:29:30.940 --> 01:29:34.950
A Q Drum you can
almost find nowhere.

01:29:34.950 --> 01:29:37.530
And if you think about
shipping a five gallon bucket,

01:29:37.530 --> 01:29:39.900
they stack into
each other so you

01:29:39.900 --> 01:29:41.670
can fit a whole lot
more five gallon

01:29:41.670 --> 01:29:45.150
buckets in a shipping
container than you can Q Drums.

01:29:45.150 --> 01:29:47.460
And that makes the Q Drum
a whole lot more expensive

01:29:47.460 --> 01:29:51.360
because shipping costs get
very high very quickly.

01:29:51.360 --> 01:29:53.490
Any other thoughts or concerns?

01:29:57.020 --> 01:30:01.190
One of the biggest ones when
this technology came out

01:30:01.190 --> 01:30:04.820
was they used pictures like
this one to advertise it,

01:30:04.820 --> 01:30:06.950
and people were
horrified because kids

01:30:06.950 --> 01:30:09.332
were carrying water.

01:30:09.332 --> 01:30:10.790
Which is a really
interesting thing

01:30:10.790 --> 01:30:14.280
to think about because kids
are already carrying water,

01:30:14.280 --> 01:30:18.110
so it's not actually a
very sensible concern

01:30:18.110 --> 01:30:23.430
from the approach of
what is really happening.

01:30:23.430 --> 01:30:26.240
But from a where are you
getting your money from concern,

01:30:26.240 --> 01:30:28.760
it's huge, because if you
alienate all your donors

01:30:28.760 --> 01:30:31.642
by showing them an
improved reality but they

01:30:31.642 --> 01:30:34.100
aren't aware of the real reality
and you don't educate them

01:30:34.100 --> 01:30:37.100
well, you destroy
your donor pool.

01:30:37.100 --> 01:30:38.720
And if you're going
off of donations,

01:30:38.720 --> 01:30:42.020
which you'd have to do in a Q
Drum because it's so expensive,

01:30:42.020 --> 01:30:43.230
that's a real problem.

01:30:43.230 --> 01:30:45.920
And so I think that's one of
the most interesting aspects

01:30:45.920 --> 01:30:50.030
of the Q Drum for me
is it seems so great.

01:30:50.030 --> 01:30:52.190
Kids are dragging water
instead of carrying it,

01:30:52.190 --> 01:30:53.340
what a huge improvement.

01:30:53.340 --> 01:30:55.280
But if your donors
are assuming now

01:30:55.280 --> 01:30:57.080
this is allowing
children to carry

01:30:57.080 --> 01:31:00.110
water who used to be in school,
then all of a sudden, no one

01:31:00.110 --> 01:31:01.860
will support you.

01:31:01.860 --> 01:31:05.430
So it's a really interesting
aspect of this type of work.

01:31:05.430 --> 01:31:07.250
And then just to check
in on where we are,

01:31:07.250 --> 01:31:09.980
today we did lighting and
community partner introduction.

01:31:09.980 --> 01:31:11.570
The lab will be Friday.

01:31:11.570 --> 01:31:14.870
Next week will be
solar thermal and PV

01:31:14.870 --> 01:31:19.430
and the quiz in the flip
order is what we just decided.

01:31:19.430 --> 01:31:25.170
And then-- yeah, this
is where we're at.

